type DashboardActuationPosture
  = DashboardObserveOnly
  | DashboardActuating

type DashboardProvisionTransport
  = DashboardLocalProvision
  | DashboardSshProvision { host: NonEmptyStr }

type DashboardSourceSeed
  = DashboardExistingRepo { anchor_ref: NonEmptyStr }
  | DashboardGitBundle {
      controller_repo: FilePath,
      staged_bundle: FilePath,
      anchor_ref: NonEmptyStr,
      history_remote: NonEmptyStr,
    }

// THE ADMITTED BINDING IS A FIELD, NOT A CHECK SOMEONE MAY RUN. gunbc.provider_account models which
// account a credential belongs to and how many concurrent sessions its persistence behaviour can
// carry, and its admission function refuses the combinations that cannot hold — but this record
// used to carry only a root and an executable, so every instance in the fleet was built along a
// path that admission never saw. The model said the invalid architecture was unwritable while the
// constructor two files away wrote it four times.\n\nAdding the binding as a required field is what
// closes it. There is no default and no second constructor, so a provider state cannot come into
// existence without naming an admitted binding, and the account a deployment uses is no longer a
// bare path that happens to end in a directory name — it is a selection the admission agreed
// to.\n\nThe four declarations below bind ONE session each, which is what a serve process is, and
// take the total single-session path admitted_single_session_binding provides. That path cannot
// refuse, because a single session is admissible under every discipline resting on evidence; what
// it cannot do is accept an unobserved credential, since EvidencedSharingDiscipline has no unknown
// arm to pass it. A deployment wanting more than one session goes through the partial admission and
// must match its refusal.

type DashboardProviderStateBinding
  = DashboardExistingProviderState {
      root: FilePath,
      executable: FilePath?,
      binding: AdmittedAccountBinding,
    }

// WHOSE STATE THE DISPATCH ROOTS ARE IS A DECLARATION, NOT AN INFERENCE, and it is the axis this
// record was missing when blue/green landed. An instance owns two roots a dispatch WRITES — the
// worktree root and the published attempt-state root — and until now both were derived from
// instance_root for every instance, which silently answered a question nobody had asked: that
// dispatch evidence belongs to the SLOT.\n\nFor an isolated lab that answer is right and
// load-bearing. A lab exists to be disjoint from production, and handing it the host-shared roots
// would let one lab's apply or retract disturb another lab's live worktrees and published attempts.
// For a production blue/green SLOT the same answer is wrong, and wrong in two directions at once.
// The cutover retracts the retiring slot, so slot-scoped roots put a teardown of live dispatch
// evidence on the per-push path — the empty-directory wall then refuses, correctly, and every main
// push reds (measured 2026-08-01: seven consecutive deploy jobs, alternating by candidate, both
// directions failing once each slot had served long enough to accumulate evidence). And because
// each slot reads its OWN roots, a flip moves the dashboard to a process that cannot see the
// attempts dispatched under its predecessor.\n\nThe defect is not that either answer is wrong. It
// is that ONE derivation answered for both kinds of instance, so the two could not disagree. Making
// it a field with no default means an instance cannot come into existence without stating which it
// is, and the two laws below read the declaration rather than guessing from instance_root,
// instance_id, or actuation posture — none of which separate a lab from a production slot, since
// labs and slots are both DashboardActuating.\n\nTHIS DOES NOT REOPEN
// isolated_instance_own_binary_note (operator ruling, 2026-07-30). That ruling is about
// serve_binary and its argument is mutual exclusion of PROCESSES: a lab testing a new seed must not
// overwrite the binary production executes, and retract tears Owned members down. Nothing here
// touches serve_binary, which stays instance-derived for labs and instance-declared for production
// under both scopes. The two rulings partition cleanly because they are about different members
// with opposite sharing requirements — the binary must NOT be shared because a retract would delete
// a running program, and the dispatch state MUST be shared across production slots because a flip
// would otherwise orphan live agent sessions.
//
// WHAT THE 2026-08-19 SINGLE-DEPLOYMENT CUT DID TO THIS NOTE, recorded here rather than left as
// prose arguing from a mechanism that no longer exists. Everything above about the CUTOVER -- the
// retiring slot, the per-push teardown of live dispatch evidence, the flip that moves the dashboard
// to a process blind to its predecessor's attempts -- describes events that can no longer occur:
// blue/green was deleted at the root and there is one deployment. The seven-deploy measurement
// stands as the history that produced the field and is kept for that reason, not as a live hazard.
//
// The FIELD survives with a real meaning, which is why this is prose repair and not a model change:
// it still separates an isolated lab from a production instance, and that distinction is
// load-bearing for exactly the reason the lab paragraph gives. But one consequence must be declared
// rather than discovered. For srv1_live_dashboard_instance the host-shared roots
// /opt/gunbc/dispatch-worktrees and /opt/gunbc/attempt-state are UNDER its own instance_root
// /opt/gunbc, so DispatchStateHostShared and DispatchStateInstanceOwned are now observationally
// identical on the only production instance. The containment-exemption law therefore holds over
// ZERO declared subjects, and its witness
// (host_shared_slots_are_exempt_from_containment_but_labs_are_not) had to move to a counterfactual
// slot to stay discriminating. That is honest but weak: the law is enforced only against a
// constructed instance, so a real production instance whose roots stopped being host-shared would
// not be caught by it. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: a second production instance on any host, at which point
// the exemption regains a declared subject and the witness returns to the live population.

// THE SCOPE AND THE ROOTS TRAVEL TOGETHER, because as three independent fields they could disagree
// and the model could only NOTICE that afterwards (codex review 47405, #7637 follow-up). The first
// cut declared a bare two-variant scope beside dispatch_worktree_root and
// dispatch_attempt_state_root, so a slot could be labelled host-shared while carrying roots derived
// from its own instance root — exactly the state the change existed to eliminate. The tell was in
// the evidence: the RED witness had to CONSTRUCT that combination to prove the law caught it, which
// is DESIGN §4b rung two, mechanically preventable, standing where rung four was
// available.\n\nCarrying the roots in the variant moves it up. An instance-owned scope carries the
// CHILD NAMES its roots hang under, and the roots are derived beneath instance_root; a host-shared
// scope carries the layout roots themselves. There is no third field to contradict either, so the
// MISLABELLED slot — host-shared in name while its roots derive from its own instance root — has no
// representation rather than a check that finds it.
//
// BE EXACT ABOUT WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLOSE, because overstating a rung is worse than sitting at it
// (DESIGN §4b rung honesty). A host-shared variant still carries two free FilePath values, so a
// production slot CAN be authored with roots that are not the layout rows. That state remains
// representable and dashboard_instances_dispatch_state_is_consistent is what refuses it —
// mechanically preventable, not structurally impossible — and
// a_production_slot_keeping_its_own_dispatch_state_is_refused is the live discriminating red
// proving the law still executes. What DID climb is the instance-owned side, which carries child
// names only and therefore cannot reach outside its instance root at all. Next-rung trigger, so
// this is a tracked stall rather than a silent one: host-shared roots DERIVED from a total
// host-identity-to-layout lookup instead of carried. Dissolve-on
// feature:host-dispatch-roots-derived.\n\nTHE CHILD NAMES ARE CARRIED RATHER THAN FIXED, and that
// is not incidental: production hangs its worktrees under dispatch-worktrees while isolated labs
// use attempts. A uniform derivation would have silently RENAMED production's live worktree root —
// the kind of quiet migration a refactor produces when it assumes regularity the corpus does not
// have.

type DispatchStateScope
  = DispatchStateInstanceOwned {
      worktree_child: NonEmptyStr,
      attempt_state_child: NonEmptyStr,
    }
  | DispatchStateHostShared {
      worktree_root: FilePath,
      attempt_state_root: FilePath,
    }

// deployment IDENTIFIES THE CONVERGENCE SUBJECT THIS INSTANCE REALIZES (gunbc.deployment_risk). It is a
// required field so every constructor answers it; the instance's risk class is DERIVED from it
// through prod_role_selection (dashboard_instance_risk_class) and never from instance_id, host,
// or a constructor's name -- srv1_live is a migration-era name and determines nothing.
type HostDashboardInstance {
  deployment: DeploymentId
  instance_id: NonEmptyStr
  host_identity: NonEmptyStr
  provision_parent_root: FilePath
  instance_root: FilePath
  local_origin_root: FilePath
  local_origin_publish_ref: NonEmptyStr
  repo_root: FilePath
  dispatch_base_ref: NonEmptyStr
  publication_base_branch: NonEmptyStr
  dispatch_state_scope: DispatchStateScope
  execution_environment: DispatchHostEnvironment
  provider_state: DashboardProviderStateBinding
  serve_binary: FilePath
  serve_function: NonEmptyStr
  listen_host: NonEmptyStr
  listen_port: Port
  tmux_server: TmuxServerScope
  actuation: DashboardActuationPosture
  provision_transport: DashboardProvisionTransport
  source_seed: DashboardSourceSeed
  admitted_revision: DashboardAdmittedRevisionSource
  toolchain: DashboardToolchain
  dispatch_worker_memory_max: ByteSize
  directory_owner: PosixOwnerNames
  // THE ACCOUNT A DISPATCHED WORKER UNIT RUNS AS. Equal to directory_owner is the STAGED form of a
  // replacement migration (DESIGN section 3, gap-intolerant boundary): the worker as the service user is
  // X, a worker as a dedicated account with no root-equivalent group is Y. Y runs in shadow on the srv1
  // lab, which opts in; srv1 prod keeps WorkerIsServiceUser until an operator qualifies the
  // lab and decides the cutover, at which point this field is deleted and the worker is always the
  // dedicated account. The group of a distinct worker_user is the shared attempt group.
  worker: WorkerIdentity
  materialization_store_root: MaterializationStoreRoot?
}

// THE TOOLCHAIN IS CARRIED, NOT ONLY CONSUMED, because provisioning needs it too and provisioning
// does not go through the capability set.
//
// execution_environment answers "which programs may a DISPATCH run", and its members are checked by
// preflight before an attempt starts. Creating the instance's own roots happens BEFORE any of that
// -- it is what makes the transition gate answerable at all -- so the directory program cannot come
// from a capability the dispatch preflight owns without putting a provisioning tool in a dispatch
// contract. It comes from the toolchain instead, which is the machine-level fact both consume.
//
// KNOWN RESIDUE, stated rather than hidden: execution_environment is DERIVED from this same
// toolchain at each constructor, so the two fields can be made to disagree by passing one
// toolchain and an environment built from another. No constructor does, and each names one
// toolchain value twice in adjacent lines. The structural fix is for execution_environment to stop
// being a field and become a function of this one, which is a 37-site repoint of `.execution_
// environment` reads and does not belong in a change about serving a workspace locally.
// Dissolution trigger: that repoint. Until then this is one authority with a derived copy beside
// it, which is a weaker guarantee than one authority alone and is recorded as such.

// WHICH AUTHORITY ADMITS A REVISION FOR THIS INSTANCE -- a required field, so that no instance can
// exist without answering it.
//
// The launch gate asks whether the tree an instance dispatches from is the ADMITTED revision, and
// until the fleet-desired withdrawal the answer came from refs/fleet/desired; it is now origin/main. That
// is the right authority for a fleet host and the wrong question for a development checkout, which
// is not a fleet member and whose HEAD is ahead of the admitted fleet revision by construction --
// that being the entire point of a development checkout.
//
// The refusal that must NOT be built here is the exempting one. "Skip the revision gate for local
// instances" is a toggle whose only effect is to proceed as if the refusal had not fired, which is
// the escape hatch DESIGN 5 names; it would also make the gate untestable on the one machine a
// human iterates on. So the gate keeps its exact form -- HEAD compared against a named ref, drift
// refuses, unobserved refuses -- and only the NAMED REF varies. A development instance points at a
// ref in its own repository that the operator advances deliberately; dispatching from a tree nobody
// admitted stays refused, and admitting your own tree becomes a visible act with a receipt in the
// reflog rather than a bypass with no trace.
//
// It is a record rather than a coproduct because both fields are genuinely free: the pair is
// (which remote advertises it, which ref it is), and a closed variant set would have to be widened
// for every future admitting authority while carrying no fact the pair does not already carry.
type DashboardAdmittedRevisionSource {
  remote: String
  ref_name: GitRef
}

// THE INSTANCE'S RISK CLASS, derived through the one switchable row. Nothing else may answer it.
fn dashboard_instance_risk_class(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> DeploymentRiskClass {
  deployment_risk_class(selection: prod_role_selection, deployment: instance.deployment)
}

// The fleet's authority is origin/main: a revision is admitted when it has landed on the default
// branch. Named here rather than spelled at each instance so the fleet answer stays one value.
data fleet_admitted_revision_source: DashboardAdmittedRevisionSource = DashboardAdmittedRevisionSource {
  remote: "origin",
  ref_name: fleet_landed_main_ref_name as GitRef,
}

// A dashboard is an explicit host instance, not an ambient serve process. One value moves the
// source repository, local Git origin, attempt-worktree root, semantic execution-capability
// realizations, provider state binding, tmux server, listen address, handler entrypoint, and
// actuation posture together. Selection is by the serve entry function that receives this value; no
// environment variable may replace one projection independently. Production uses the user's default
// tmux server for compatibility. Labs and previews use named tmux servers and roots disjoint from
// production. Provider credentials remain pre-existing user-authored state and are referenced,
// never copied into a deployment payload. Capability realizations name exact executable paths and
// identity probes; observe-only means absence refuses admission until a separate
// capability-specific provisioner exists, never that generic Ensure guesses an installer.

data srv1_dashboard_live_id: NonEmptyStr = "srv1-live" as NonEmptyStr
data srv1_dashboard_lab_id: NonEmptyStr = "srv1-lab-codex-feedback-v0" as NonEmptyStr
data srv2_dashboard_preview_id: NonEmptyStr = "srv2-preview-codex-feedback-v0" as NonEmptyStr

// THE SRV1 LAB LIVES OUTSIDE THE HOME TREE. Its unit runs as the dedicated worker uid
// (WorkerDedicatedAccount), and every path the unit is handed sits under this root, so each ancestor
// must be enterable by that uid. The previous root sat under /home/briansrls/.local/share, which is
// 0710 briansrls:briansrls and which the corpus rules must not be opened (srv1 lab, 2026-10-21: the
// unit failed status=200/CHDIR). The convention it inhabits is /opt/gunbc*: gunbc.host.host_layout
// roots srv1's repo, bins and attempt state under /opt/gunbc, so the lab is the sibling /opt/gunbc-lab
// rather than a new /var/lib family. /opt/gunbc-lab is created by the deploy's owned-directory route
// (dashboard_instance_provision_parent_directory) and the instance root beneath it is
// briansrls:gunbc-attempt 0750 (dashboard_instance_root_directory): the shared group traverses by
// membership and other gets nothing. Reachability is established before a unit starts by
// gunbc.roadmap.dispatch_worker_traversal.
data srv1_dashboard_lab_provision_parent: FilePath = "/opt/gunbc-lab" as FilePath
data srv1_dashboard_lab_root: FilePath = "/opt/gunbc-lab/codex-provider-feedback-v0" as FilePath
// srv2's roots stay under the home tree on purpose: the preview is DashboardObserveOnly and the srv2
// lab is a WorkerIsServiceUser instance, so neither runs a dedicated worker uid and no foreign uid is
// handed a path under them. They move when an srv2 instance adopts WorkerDedicatedAccount.
data dashboard_home_provision_parent_root: FilePath = "/home/briansrls/.local/share" as FilePath
data srv2_dashboard_preview_root: FilePath = "/home/briansrls/.local/share/gunbc-dashboard-instances/codex-provider-feedback-preview-v0" as FilePath
// DERIVED FROM THE TOOL AUTHORITY, NOT A SECOND DECLARATION OF IT. This row is a product-layer
// NAME for a fact that belongs to env(1): which absolute path this deployment's capability
// bindings exec. It used to be the only place in the repository where env's path was declared at
// all, which made a product module the upstream authority for a coreutils binary -- a section-3
// layer inversion sitting underneath the nickname. extdeps.tools.env owns the fact now; the name
// survives here because the binding surface reads better for it, and because a name that resolves
// to the authority is not a fork.
data dashboard_environment_binding_executable: FilePath = env_absolute_program().invocation as String as FilePath

fn dashboard_instance_child(root: FilePath, child: String) -> FilePath {
  join([root as String, "/", child], "") as FilePath
}

// THE INSTANCE ROOT IS ABSOLUTE AND SPELLED ONE WAY, a fact of the instance that every store derived from
// instance_root reads, so it is answered here once and not per store: one rule, applied at every sibling store.
// A relative root resolves against each process's working directory, so two processes would address two
// directories as "the" instance; "/" is the host root, not an instance. A doubled or trailing separator, or
// a `.` or `..` segment, still names the same directory on disk, but every identity this repository takes by
// string reads it as another: gunbc.durable_exclusive_hold_file_store file_hold_ref (a release over the
// other spelling refuses as ForAnotherHold), dashboard_declared_instance_roots_are_unique (two rows naming
// one directory would pass) and dashboard_instance_for_instance_root. NOT ESTABLISHED: that no component is a
// symlink; the filesystem interface has no resolution operation, so that rests on the declaration.
fn dashboard_instance_root_spelling_refusal(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> String? {
  let root = instance.instance_root as String
  let segments = split(s: root, delimiter: "/")
  if !starts_with(s: root, prefix: "/") {
    Present { value: "it is not absolute" }
  } else if root == "/" {
    Present { value: "it is the filesystem root" }
  } else if any(segments.skip(n: 1), s => s == "" || s == "." || s == "..") {
    Present { value: "it has an empty, `.` or `..` segment (a doubled or trailing separator, or a relative step)" }
  } else {
    none
  }
}
// THE INSTANCE'S TAILNET DOOR: the unix socket its door unit serves on and tailscale serve proxies
// to. It sits in a directory of its own under the instance root, which the deployment ensures owned
// by the service principal: the instance root itself need not be the principal's to write (srv1's
// /opt/gunbc is root-owned 0755 and the deploy re-asserts it), so a socket bound directly in it
// could not be created and readiness, which requires the door, could never hold (srv1 precondition
// sweep, 2026-10-10) -- the defect roadmap_event_carrier_directory answered for the event worktree.
// Who may open the socket is not what the door relies on -- the handler is handed the
// kernel-attested peer of every connection (`gunbc serve --unix-socket`, peer_user) and only
// tailscaled's is the proxy.
fn dashboard_instance_tailnet_door_directory(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> OwnedDirectoryTarget {
  dashboard_instance_shared_directory(instance: instance, member: "tailnet-door" as NonEmptyStr, child: "tailnet-door")
}

fn dashboard_instance_tailnet_door_socket(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join([owned_directory_path(t: dashboard_instance_tailnet_door_directory(instance: instance)) as String, "/door.sock"], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

fn dashboard_instance_provider_state_root(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> FilePath {
  match instance.provider_state {
    DashboardExistingProviderState { root, executable: _, binding: _ } => root
  }
}

fn dashboard_instance_provider_executable(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> FilePath? {
  match instance.provider_state {
    DashboardExistingProviderState { root: _, executable, binding: _ } => executable
  }
}

fn dashboard_instance_provider_executable_matches(
  executable: FilePath,
  instance: HostDashboardInstance,
) -> Bool {
  match dashboard_instance_provider_executable(instance: instance) {
    Present { value: expected } => executable == expected
    Absent => false
  }
}

fn dashboard_instance_provider_executable_refusal_detail(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> String {
  join([
    "bundled Codex executable path unavailable for host ",
    instance.host_identity as String,
    "; fail-closed — committed lock placement not realized",
  ], "")
}

fn dashboard_instance_capability_executable(
  instance: HostDashboardInstance,
  cap: ExecutionCapability,
) -> FilePath? {
  match dispatch_host_realization(
    environment: instance.execution_environment,
    capability: cap,
  ) {
    Present { value: realization } =>
      Present { value: realization.executable }
    Absent => none
  }
}

fn dashboard_instance_dispatch_platform_executables(
  instance: HostDashboardInstance,
) -> List<FilePath> {
  dispatch_platform_required_capabilities()
    |> flat_map(cap =>
      match dashboard_instance_capability_executable(
        instance: instance,
        cap: cap,
      ) {
        Present { value: executable } => [executable]
        Absent => []
      })
}

fn dashboard_instance_ownership_marker_path(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> FilePath {
  dashboard_ownership_marker_in(root: instance.instance_root, instance_id: instance.instance_id)
}

// THE OWNERSHIP MARKER'S ONE NAMING RULE: an empty file under a root, named for the instance that
// owns the root. The instance root carries it (above), and so does the prod-role holder's fabric
// store control directory (gunbc.fabric.fabric_storage_placement fabric_storage_ownership_marker_path), so an
// old store with no unit is still observably someone's state rather than reading as nothing.
fn dashboard_ownership_marker_in(root: FilePath, instance_id: NonEmptyStr) -> FilePath {
  dashboard_instance_child(
    root: root,
    child: join([".gunbc-dashboard-instance-", instance_id as String], ""),
  )
}

fn dashboard_instance_checkout_branch(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> NonEmptyStr {
  replace(
    s: instance.local_origin_publish_ref as String,
    from: "refs/heads/",
    to: "",
  ) as NonEmptyStr
}

fn dashboard_instance_controller_workdir(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> FilePath {
  match instance.source_seed {
    DashboardExistingRepo { anchor_ref: _ } => instance.repo_root
    DashboardGitBundle {
      controller_repo,
      staged_bundle: _,
      anchor_ref: _,
      history_remote: _,
    } =>
      controller_repo
  }
}

fn dashboard_instance_serve_argv(
  instance: HostDashboardInstance,
  release_revision: String,
) -> List<String> {
  [
    "serve",
    "--source-root", "dag",
    "--source-root", "src/v2",
    "--entry", "dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_serve.dag",
    "--function", instance.serve_function as String,
    "--host", instance.listen_host as String,
    "--port", to_string(instance.listen_port),
    "--release-revision", release_revision,
  ]
}

fn dashboard_actuation_posture_label(posture: DashboardActuationPosture) -> String {
  match posture {
    DashboardObserveOnly => "observe-only"
    DashboardActuating => "actuating"
  }
}

fn dashboard_provision_transport_label(transport: DashboardProvisionTransport) -> String {
  match transport {
    DashboardLocalProvision => "local"
    DashboardSshProvision { host } => join(["ssh:", host as String], "")
  }
}

data dashboard_instance_health_schema: String = "roadmap-dashboard-instance/v1"

// The served health document leads with schema then instance_id, so a reader can identify it
// POSITIONALLY instead of scanning the body — the same rule
// dispatch_environment_receipt_identity_prefix states for the admission receipt. A health reader
// that scanned for an instance_id fragment would accept any document carrying it in any nested
// value, which is how a foreign or stale server on the polled port fabricates a converged apply.

fn dashboard_instance_health_identity_prefix(instance_id: String) -> String {
  serialize_json_object_prefix(members: [
    json_kv(key: "schema", value: json_string(s: dashboard_instance_health_schema)),
    json_kv(key: "instance_id", value: json_string(s: instance_id)),
  ])
}

fn dashboard_named_tmux_server(instance_id: NonEmptyStr) -> TmuxServerScope {
  NamedTmuxServer { socket_name: instance_id }
}

// ONE CAPABILITY SET, N BOUND TOOLCHAINS -- the DESIGN 3 split between the interface shape and its
// realization, applied to the executables a dispatch host runs.
//
// The capability LIST, each capability's identity probe, and each ensure policy are facts about
// what a dispatch host must be able to do; WHERE those programs live is a fact about the machine.
// Fusing them, as the single srv1-shaped producer did, made every path a Linux path by
// construction: a second host could only be served by copying twelve rows and editing the paths,
// which is the redundancy DESIGN 2 prices and the fork DESIGN 3 forbids -- the two copies drift
// apart at exactly the moment a capability is added, because nobody edits the copy they are not
// looking at.
//
// So the toolchain is the parameter and the capability set is the invariant. Adding a platform is
// one DashboardToolchain value; adding a CAPABILITY is one row here that every platform must then
// answer for, which is the direction the failure should point.
type DashboardToolchain {
  directory_create: FilePath
  path_test: FilePath
  path_stat: FilePath
  path_list: FilePath
  git: FilePath
  tmux: FilePath
  pipe_shell: FilePath
  tee: FilePath
  jq: FilePath
  environment_binding: FilePath
  install: FilePath
  link: FilePath
  timeout: FilePath
  systemd_run: FilePath?
  cargo: FilePath
  rustc: FilePath
  rustfmt: FilePath
}

// The GNU identity strings below are not cosmetic and the macOS toolchain is why they are worth
// stating. The attempt-pointer argv passes `ln -sfnT`, and `-T` does not exist in BSD ln; the
// attempt-state argv passes `install -d -m`, which BSD install does happen to support. So the
// identity probe asserting "coreutils" is load-bearing on both hosts: it is the executed check that
// the program bound here answers the GNU contract the argv is written against, and on a Mac the
// system /bin/ln would fail that probe BEFORE it could fail the flag at spawn time. That is the
// difference between a typed preflight refusal naming the capability and a dispatch that dies
// mid-attempt on an illegal option.
// THE CEILING ONE DISPATCHED ATTEMPT MAY OCCUPY, DERIVED FROM THE SLOT AUTHORITY RATHER THAN
// AUTHORED HERE. The first cut of this row was `byte_size(count: 17179869184)` with a hand
// justification about observed peaks, and that was the section 3 violation in its purest form: the
// number is EXACTLY gunbc_runner_slot_desired().memory_max, which is not a coincidence to be
// noted but an authority to be consumed. That row carries an operator ruling derived from 186
// floor-job cgroup peaks, a superseded prior derivation retained as method, and its own dissolve-on
// -- none of which a literal here inherits, and all of which a literal here would silently
// contradict the moment the ruling moves.
//
// THE REUSE IS ON PROVEN COINCIDENCE, NOT ON A MATCHING NUMBER. What the slot row measured is
// floor-job cgroup peaks, and a floor job is a gunbc process resolving this corpus. A dispatched
// harness worker is the same workload shape -- one `gunbc run` resolving the same corpus -- so the
// distribution the ruling derived from is the distribution this ceiling faces. Two concepts that
// merely happened to share a number would have to stay separate; these share the measurement.
//
// WHERE THIS IS POINTED, and it is deliberately NOT built yet. A per-attempt ceiling is one half of
// a concurrency question whose other half already exists: gunbc.runner.runner_slot_allocation derives
// host_memory_admitted_width, host_cpu_admitted_width and host_disk_admitted_width and commits the
// MINIMUM over those axes, which is exactly "the most concurrent fraction this host admits", and
// gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_budget already expresses shares as fractions over a measure
// (measure_scale_fraction_floor) and charges control-plane residency against what a host may
// commit. Dispatch consumes none of it: nothing today asks how many attempts this host admits
// before starting one, so concurrency is bounded by whoever is clicking rather than by the budget.
//
// DISSOLUTION TRIGGER: dispatch admission takes a width from the same minimum-over-axes derivation
// the runner slots use, at which point this row stops being a per-attempt ceiling standing alone
// and becomes one term in an allocation the fabric can express. Until then it is honest about being
// a ceiling with no width beside it.
data dispatch_worker_memory_ceiling: ByteSize = gunbc_runner_slot_desired().memory_max

data srv1_linux_toolchain: DashboardToolchain = DashboardToolchain {
  directory_create: "/usr/bin/mkdir" as FilePath,
  path_test: "/usr/bin/test" as FilePath,
  path_stat: "/usr/bin/stat" as FilePath,
  path_list: "/usr/bin/ls" as FilePath,
  git: srv1_software_inhabitance_receipt.git_path,
  tmux: "/usr/bin/tmux" as FilePath,
  pipe_shell: "/usr/bin/sh" as FilePath,
  tee: "/usr/bin/tee" as FilePath,
  jq: srv1_software_inhabitance_receipt.jq_path,
  environment_binding: dashboard_environment_binding_executable,
  install: "/usr/bin/install" as FilePath,
  link: "/usr/bin/ln" as FilePath,
  timeout: srv1_software_inhabitance_receipt.timeout_path,
  systemd_run: Present { value: srv1_software_inhabitance_receipt.systemd_run_path },
  cargo: "/home/briansrls/.cargo/bin/cargo" as FilePath,
  rustc: "/home/briansrls/.cargo/bin/rustc" as FilePath,
  rustfmt: "/home/briansrls/.cargo/bin/rustfmt" as FilePath,
}

// THE OPERATOR MACBOOK, OBSERVED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. Every path here was read off the machine on
// 2026-09-02 with `which` and each identity probe was executed, because a toolchain row is a claim
// about a specific host and an unrun claim is the fabricated-plausible-output failure in its
// cheapest form. What the probes established, and why the rows do not simply mirror srv1:
// tmux, cargo, rustc and rustfmt are Homebrew rather than system or rustup; sh is /bin/sh, since
// macOS has no /usr/bin/sh at all; and tee, install, ln and env are the g-prefixed GNU builds,
// because the system BSD ones answer no --version and, for ln, implement no -T.
data macbook_local_toolchain: DashboardToolchain = DashboardToolchain {
  directory_create: "/bin/mkdir" as FilePath,
  path_test: "/bin/test" as FilePath,
  path_stat: "/opt/homebrew/bin/gstat" as FilePath,
  path_list: "/opt/homebrew/bin/gls" as FilePath,
  git: "/usr/bin/git" as FilePath,
  tmux: "/opt/homebrew/bin/tmux" as FilePath,
  pipe_shell: "/bin/sh" as FilePath,
  tee: "/opt/homebrew/bin/gtee" as FilePath,
  jq: "/usr/bin/jq" as FilePath,
  environment_binding: "/opt/homebrew/bin/genv" as FilePath,
  install: "/opt/homebrew/bin/ginstall" as FilePath,
  link: "/opt/homebrew/bin/gln" as FilePath,
  timeout: "/opt/homebrew/bin/gtimeout" as FilePath,
  systemd_run: none,
  cargo: "/opt/homebrew/bin/cargo" as FilePath,
  rustc: "/opt/homebrew/bin/rustc" as FilePath,
  rustfmt: "/opt/homebrew/bin/rustfmt" as FilePath,
}

fn dashboard_execution_environment_for_toolchain(
  toolchain: DashboardToolchain,
  serve_binary: FilePath,
) -> DispatchHostEnvironment {
  DispatchHostEnvironment {
    realizations: [
      CapabilityRealization {
        capability: GitWorkspaceCapability,
        executable: toolchain.git,
        identity_args: ["--version"],
        identity_contains: "git version" as NonEmptyStr,
        ensure_policy: EnsureObserveOnly,
      },
      CapabilityRealization {
        capability: TmuxSessionCapability,
        executable: toolchain.tmux,
        identity_args: ["-V"],
        identity_contains: "tmux" as NonEmptyStr,
        ensure_policy: EnsureObserveOnly,
      },
      CapabilityRealization {
        capability: TmuxPipeShellCapability,
        executable: toolchain.pipe_shell,
        identity_args: ["-c", "printf gunbc-tmux-pipe-shell"],
        identity_contains: "gunbc-tmux-pipe-shell" as NonEmptyStr,
        ensure_policy: EnsureObserveOnly,
      },
      CapabilityRealization {
        capability: TeeEventCaptureCapability,
        executable: toolchain.tee,
        identity_args: ["--version"],
        identity_contains: "coreutils" as NonEmptyStr,
        ensure_policy: EnsureObserveOnly,
      },
      CapabilityRealization {
        capability: ProviderEventProjectionCapability,
        executable: toolchain.jq,
        identity_args: ["--version"],
        identity_contains: "jq-" as NonEmptyStr,
        ensure_policy: EnsureObserveOnly,
      },
      CapabilityRealization {
        capability: EnvironmentBindingCapability,
        executable: toolchain.environment_binding,
        identity_args: ["--version"],
        identity_contains: "coreutils" as NonEmptyStr,
        ensure_policy: EnsureObserveOnly,
      },
      CapabilityRealization {
        capability: AttemptStateDirectoryCapability,
        executable: toolchain.install,
        identity_args: ["--version"],
        identity_contains: "coreutils" as NonEmptyStr,
        ensure_policy: EnsureObserveOnly,
      },
      CapabilityRealization {
        capability: AttemptCurrentPointerCapability,
        executable: toolchain.link,
        identity_args: ["--version"],
        identity_contains: "coreutils" as NonEmptyStr,
        ensure_policy: EnsureObserveOnly,
      },
      CapabilityRealization {
        capability: GunbcClaimRunnerCapability,
        executable: serve_binary,
        identity_args: ["--help"],
        identity_contains: "A causal compiler" as NonEmptyStr,
        ensure_policy: EnsureObserveOnly,
      },
      CapabilityRealization {
        capability: CargoCapability,
        executable: toolchain.cargo,
        identity_args: ["--version"],
        identity_contains: "cargo" as NonEmptyStr,
        ensure_policy: EnsureObserveOnly,
      },
      CapabilityRealization {
        capability: RustcCapability,
        executable: toolchain.rustc,
        identity_args: ["--version"],
        identity_contains: "rustc" as NonEmptyStr,
        ensure_policy: EnsureObserveOnly,
      },
      CapabilityRealization {
        capability: RustfmtCapability,
        executable: toolchain.rustfmt,
        identity_args: ["--version"],
        identity_contains: "rustfmt" as NonEmptyStr,
        ensure_policy: EnsureObserveOnly,
      },
    ],
  }
}

fn dashboard_execution_environment(
  serve_binary: FilePath,
) -> DispatchHostEnvironment {
  dashboard_execution_environment_for_toolchain(
    toolchain: srv1_linux_toolchain,
    serve_binary: serve_binary,
  )
}

// THE repo_root -> INSTANCE RESOLUTION LIVES WITH THE INSTANCES, NOT WITH ONE CONSUMER. It was
// authored inside gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_belt_actuate as belt_dashboard_instance_lookup_for_spawn_workdir,
// which was correct while the belt tick was the only caller and stopped being correct the moment a
// second one existed: the mapping is a fact about which host instances this repository declares,
// and a consumer that re-derives it forks an authority rather than consuming one (DESIGN 3, consume
// never fork). Copying the shape and noting the copy in a comment does not repair that — the two
// spellings drift apart exactly when an instance is added, which is the one moment nobody is
// looking at either of them.
//
// The name lost its belt vocabulary with the move. `spawn workdir` is the belt's framing of the
// argument it happens to pass; the fact being asked for is `which declared instance has this repo
// root`, and naming it that way is what lets the preflight ask the same question without reading as
// a borrowed belt call. The refusal arm carries the unmatched root rather than a bare Absent,
// because a caller that has just been told the root is unknown needs to name it in its own
// diagnostic.
//
// THE SET IS ONE ROSTER. Named constructors remain so a witness can name srv1-live without walking
// a list, but membership is public_builtin_dashboard_instances, and every lookup folds that list. Adding
// an instance is a constructor plus one row on that roster; host, repo-root, instance-root and id
// resolution do not get a second spelling.

type DashboardInstanceLookup
  = DashboardInstanceResolved { instance: HostDashboardInstance }
  | DashboardInstanceRootUnknown { repo_root: String }

// THE INSTANCE BY THE HOST IT RUNS ON, for a caller that knows where it is executing and not
// which checkout it was launched from: a harness turn or a belt tick reads its own short hostname
// and resolves the instance whose host_identity spells it. An unknown host is its own arm.
//
// THE ROSTER ADMITS AT MOST ONE HOST-LOOKUP ANSWER PER HOST. That is the constraint, not a
// uniqueness-of-ids corollary: two ExistingRepo deployments on one machine are a real ambiguity
// about which instance is "srv1", and dashboard_host_lookup_subjects_are_unique is the law
// that names it. A future deployment-shaped instance on a host that already has one must not
// also answer host lookup — seed it as a bundle, or give it a different host_identity, or the
// witness is the refusal.
//
// SOURCE_SEED IS A PROXY FOR THAT QUESTION, NOT THE QUESTION. "Who answers for this machine" is
// not "how was this tree seeded". They coincide in the CURRENT population because
// isolated_dashboard_instance always writes DashboardGitBundle and every instance that today
// answers for a host (srv1-live, srv2-deploy, macbook-local) is DashboardExistingRepo. That is
// coincidence of the constructors, not a structural identity. It breaks on an ExistingRepo
// lab (a dedicated clone on a host that already has a live instance — the srv1-dev shape) and
// on a bundle-seeded deployment that should answer for the machine. Until host-lookup
// answering is its own field, those two shapes cannot both be declared honestly, and this
// predicate is the stated divergence.
type DashboardInstanceByHost
  = DashboardInstanceForHost { instance: HostDashboardInstance }
  | DashboardInstanceHostUnknown { host: String }

type DashboardInstanceById
  = DashboardInstanceForId { instance: HostDashboardInstance }
  | DashboardInstanceIdUnknown { instance_id: String }

type DashboardInstanceByInstanceRoot
  = DashboardInstanceForInstanceRoot { instance: HostDashboardInstance }
  | DashboardInstanceInstanceRootUnknown { instance_root: String }

fn dashboard_instance_answers_host_lookup(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> Bool {
  match instance.source_seed {
    DashboardExistingRepo { anchor_ref: _ } => true
    DashboardGitBundle {
      controller_repo: _,
      staged_bundle: _,
      anchor_ref: _,
      history_remote: _,
    } => false
  }
}

fn dashboard_instance_for_host(host: String) -> DashboardInstanceByHost {
  match public_builtin_dashboard_instances()
    |> filter(i =>
      i.host_identity as String == host && dashboard_instance_answers_host_lookup(instance: i)
    )
    |> first
  {
    Present { value: instance } => DashboardInstanceForHost { instance: instance }
    Absent => DashboardInstanceHostUnknown { host: host }
  }
}

fn dashboard_instance_for_repo_root(repo_root: String) -> DashboardInstanceLookup {
  match public_builtin_dashboard_instances()
    |> filter(i => i.repo_root as String == repo_root)
    |> first
  {
    Present { value: instance } => DashboardInstanceResolved { instance: instance }
    Absent => DashboardInstanceRootUnknown { repo_root: repo_root }
  }
}

fn dashboard_instance_for_id(instance_id: String) -> DashboardInstanceById {
  match public_builtin_dashboard_instances()
    |> filter(i => i.instance_id as String == instance_id)
    |> first
  {
    Present { value: instance } => DashboardInstanceForId { instance: instance }
    Absent => DashboardInstanceIdUnknown { instance_id: instance_id }
  }
}

fn dashboard_instance_for_instance_root(instance_root: String) -> DashboardInstanceByInstanceRoot {
  match public_builtin_dashboard_instances()
    |> filter(i => i.instance_root as String == instance_root)
    |> first
  {
    Present { value: instance } => DashboardInstanceForInstanceRoot { instance: instance }
    Absent => DashboardInstanceInstanceRootUnknown { instance_root: instance_root }
  }
}

// THE PROD-ROLE HOLDER'S REALIZATION -- the ONE lookup from the role's DeploymentId to the
// HostDashboardInstance that realizes it (gunbc.deployment_risk). It is the instance roster
// filtered by its own deployment field, so there is no second id-to-instance table to drift.
// Every role-following singleton (approval broker owner, fabric storage placement) asks THIS, never
// srv1 by name. Under NoProdRole it REFUSES with a typed cause; there is no arm that falls back to
// srv1, and a holder with zero or several realizations is its own refusal rather than "the first".
// History: gunbc#13217 (closed, branch session/sharp-heron-165) first drew this resolver.
type ProdRoleRealizationRefusal
  = ProdRoleUnheld
  | ProdRoleHolderUnrealized { deployment: DeploymentId }
  | ProdRoleHolderAmbiguous { deployment: DeploymentId, count: Int }
  | ProdRoleHolderUnactuatable { deployment: DeploymentId }

// THE HOLDER TOKEN. sole_constructor, so only this module -- and in it only prod_role_realization_in
// -- can mint one: a role-following singleton is built FROM a holder, and no caller can claim
// ownership by spelling an instance_id or by constructing a holder for an instance of its choosing.
type ProdRoleHolder sole_constructor {
  instance: HostDashboardInstance
}

type ProdRoleRealization
  = ProdRoleRealizedBy { holder: ProdRoleHolder }
  | ProdRoleRealizationRefused { cause: ProdRoleRealizationRefusal }

fn prod_role_realization_in(
  selection: ProdRoleSelection,
  instances: List<HostDashboardInstance>,
) -> ProdRoleRealization {
  match selection {
    NoProdRole => ProdRoleRealizationRefused { cause: ProdRoleUnheld }
    ProdRoleHeldBy { deployment: holder } => {
      let realizing = instances |> filter(i => i.deployment == holder)
      match realizing |> first {
        Absent => ProdRoleRealizationRefused { cause: ProdRoleHolderUnrealized { deployment: holder } }
        Present { value: only } =>
          if count(realizing) == 1 {
            ProdRoleRealizedBy { holder: ProdRoleHolder { instance: only } }
          } else {
            ProdRoleRealizationRefused { cause: ProdRoleHolderAmbiguous { deployment: holder, count: count(realizing) } }
          }
      }
    }
  }
}

// The production question: the real switch over the real roster.
fn prod_role_realization() -> ProdRoleRealization {
  prod_role_realization_in(selection: prod_role_selection, instances: public_builtin_dashboard_instances())
}

fn prod_role_realization_refusal_detail(cause: ProdRoleRealizationRefusal) -> String {
  match cause {
    ProdRoleUnheld => "prod role refused: prod_role_selection is NoProdRole; no deployment holds it"
    ProdRoleHolderUnrealized { deployment } =>
      join(["prod role refused: holder ", deployment.name as String, " has no HostDashboardInstance"], "")
    ProdRoleHolderAmbiguous { deployment, count: n } =>
      join(["prod role refused: holder ", deployment.name as String, " has ", to_string(n), " instances"], "")
    ProdRoleHolderUnactuatable { deployment } =>
      join(["prod role refused: holder ", deployment.name as String, " has no desired deployment row (gunbc.live_deploy.desired desired_deployment_roster), so nothing can actuate it"], "")
  }
}

data macbook_local_dashboard_id: NonEmptyStr = "macbook-local" as NonEmptyStr

data macbook_operator_home_root: FilePath = "/Users/briansrls" as FilePath

data macbook_local_repo_root: FilePath = "/Users/briansrls/gunbc" as FilePath

data macbook_local_instance_root: FilePath = materialization_store_persistent_macbook_scope as FilePath

// THE DEFAULT ACCOUNT, NOT THE ROADMAP-LABELLED ONE, and the difference is observable.
//
// srv1 runs Codex under a labelled account root, ~/.codex@gunbc-roadmap, so that the dashboard's
// provider state is disjoint from any human's. This machine has no such root: the operator's Codex
// is authenticated under the plain ~/.codex that codex_account_state_root produces for
// CodexDefaultAccount. Deriving this row from codex_roadmap_account_selection produced a path that
// does not exist, and readiness refused against it -- correctly, and for a reason that reads as a
// missing login rather than as the wrong account.
//
// The selection is therefore a per-instance fact here, not a constant, and both roots come from the
// same codex_account_state_root constructor rather than one being a literal beside the other.
data macbook_local_codex_account: CodexAccountSelection = CodexDefaultAccount

data macbook_local_codex_home: FilePath = codex_account_state_root(
  home: macbook_operator_home_root,
  selection: macbook_local_codex_account,
) as String as FilePath

// THE OPERATOR MACBOOK AS A DECLARED INSTANCE, so that serving the workspace from the development
// checkout stops being a process that describes a machine it is not running on.
//
// Before this row, dashboard_instance_for_repo_root resolved exactly one root and every other
// checkout answered DashboardInstanceRootUnknown, so a locally served page reported instance
// srv1-live, host srv1 and repo /opt/gunbc/gunbc while running on a laptop. Nothing was wrong in
// srv1's own terms -- the page simply had no way to say whose it was.
//
// THREE FIELDS DIFFER FROM srv1-live FOR REASONS, not for local convenience:
//
// dispatch_state_scope is InstanceOwned rather than HostShared. srv1 puts worktrees and attempt
// state beside the deployment under /opt/gunbc; on a development machine the repository root is
// also the operator's working checkout, so host-shared roots would scatter dispatch debris through
// the tree a human is editing. Instance-owned keeps every written path under one root that can be
// removed whole.
//
// tmux_server is NAMED rather than Default. The default tmux server on this machine belongs to the
// operator, and belt observation lists sessions on the server it is given: a shared default would
// make the operator's own shells indistinguishable from dispatch sessions to the reaper. The named
// server is the same isolation the labs already declare, for the same reason.
//
// provision_transport is Local rather than ssh:srv1, which is the honest reading of a host that
// provisions itself and is why DashboardLocalProvision already exists as a variant.
//
// WHAT THIS ROW DOES NOT DO, stated because an actuating instance reads like a grant: it does not
// make dispatch possible here. The revision gate still asks whether this tree is the admitted fleet
// revision and still refuses, which is the correct answer while the admitting authority for a
// development checkout is unmodeled. This row makes the machine nameable; it does not answer for
// what may be launched on it.
fn macbook_local_dashboard_instance() -> HostDashboardInstance {
  HostDashboardInstance {
    deployment: macbook_local_deployment,
    instance_id: macbook_local_dashboard_id,
    host_identity: "macbook" as NonEmptyStr,
    provision_parent_root: "/Users/briansrls/.local/share" as FilePath,
    instance_root: macbook_local_instance_root,
    local_origin_root: macbook_local_repo_root,
    local_origin_publish_ref: "refs/heads/main" as NonEmptyStr,
    repo_root: macbook_local_repo_root,
    dispatch_base_ref: "origin/main" as NonEmptyStr,
    publication_base_branch: "main" as NonEmptyStr,
    dispatch_state_scope: DispatchStateInstanceOwned {
      worktree_child: "attempts" as NonEmptyStr,
      attempt_state_child: "attempt-state" as NonEmptyStr,
    },
    execution_environment: dashboard_execution_environment_for_toolchain(
      toolchain: macbook_local_toolchain,
      serve_binary: macbook_local_serve_binary,
    ),
    provider_state: DashboardExistingProviderState {
      root: macbook_local_codex_home,
      executable: Present { value: macbook_local_codex_executable },
      binding: admitted_single_session_binding(
        selection: macbook_local_codex_account,
        evidence: codex_roadmap_account_evidence,
      ),
    },
    serve_binary: macbook_local_serve_binary,
    serve_function: "roadmap_serve_handle" as NonEmptyStr,
    listen_host: "127.0.0.1" as NonEmptyStr,
    listen_port: 8081,
    tmux_server: dashboard_named_tmux_server(instance_id: macbook_local_dashboard_id),
    actuation: DashboardActuating,
    provision_transport: DashboardLocalProvision,
    source_seed: DashboardExistingRepo { anchor_ref: "origin/main" as NonEmptyStr },
    admitted_revision: macbook_local_admitted_revision_source,
    toolchain: macbook_local_toolchain,
    dispatch_worker_memory_max: dispatch_worker_memory_ceiling,
    directory_owner: macbook_local_directory_owner,
    worker: WorkerIsServiceUser,
    materialization_store_root: Present { value: PersistentInstanceWorkspace { scope: MacbookLocal } },
  }
}

// WHO OWNS THE INSTANCE'S DIRECTORIES, named per host because the group is a host fact: srv1's and
// srv2's service principal sits in a primary group spelled like its login (the coincidence
// gunbc.live_deploy.emit install_d_managed_command records), and macOS puts every login in `staff`.
// The owned-directory provider installs and reads back exactly this pair, so a directory created
// under another pair is refused, never re-owned.
data macbook_local_directory_owner: PosixOwnerNames = PosixOwnerNames {
  user: "briansrls" as NonEmptyStr,
  group: "staff" as NonEmptyStr,
}

fn linux_service_directory_owner() -> PosixOwnerNames {
  PosixOwnerNames {
    user: srv1_dashboard_service_principal().name,
    group: srv1_dashboard_service_principal().name,
  }
}

data macbook_local_serve_binary: FilePath = join([cargo_default_release_dir(build_root: "/Users/briansrls/gunbc"), "/gunbc"], "") as FilePath

// THE PROVIDER EXECUTABLE THIS INSTANCE OFFERS, and it is the field whose absence made the whole
// inventory empty.
//
// codex_inventory_offers_for_instance answers [] when the instance's provider executable is Absent,
// so a local dispatch that had passed every launch gate refused at selection with "no provider
// offer is selection-eligible" -- a refusal that reads like a missing harness and was a missing
// path. Worth stating because the diagnostic points at the SELECTION and the cause is in the
// INSTANCE, which is a long way to walk without a note.
//
// srv1 derives its path through codex_bundled_executable_for_host, which resolves a committed-lock
// placement for a fleet host. This machine has no such placement and needs none: Codex is installed
// here by Homebrew and already authenticated, which /provider.json reports independently. So the
// path is stated rather than derived, and the two are not a fork -- they answer the same question
// for hosts whose provisioning stories genuinely differ.
//
// This binding is expected to be SHORT-LIVED and that is not a reason to skip it. DCH-3 makes the
// dedicated harness the default selection and DCH-4 retires the vendor runtimes, at which point
// this row goes with them. It earns its place in the meantime by exercising the belt path on this
// machine -- worktree, attempt record, named-server tmux session, verification, publication -- none
// of which has ever run on macOS. Proving that against a provider known to work is what makes a
// later harness failure attributable to the harness.
data macbook_local_codex_executable: FilePath = "/opt/homebrew/bin/codex" as FilePath

// THE DEVELOPMENT CHECKOUT'S ADMITTING REF, and the deliberate act that advances it.
//
// The remote is "." -- the repository itself -- because a laptop's admitted revision is not a fact
// origin has any business advertising, and pushing a personal admission ref to a shared remote
// would make one operator's local iteration visible to every fleet host reading refs. The same
// typed ls-remote reads both: git resolves "." as a remote against the local repository, so this is
// one operation with two bindings rather than a second code path for the local case.
//
// Advancing it is `git update-ref refs/gunbc/local-admitted HEAD`, and the friction is the feature.
// An operator who has edited the tree and wants to dispatch against those edits states so; an
// operator who has not gets the same drift refusal a fleet host gets, naming both revisions. The
// reflog on this ref is the record of what was admitted and when, which a bypass flag would not
// have produced.
data macbook_local_admitted_revision_source: DashboardAdmittedRevisionSource = DashboardAdmittedRevisionSource {
  remote: ".",
  ref_name: "refs/gunbc/local-admitted" as GitRef,
}

fn srv1_live_dashboard_instance() -> HostDashboardInstance {
  HostDashboardInstance {
    deployment: srv1_daily_workspace_deployment,
    instance_id: srv1_dashboard_live_id,
    host_identity: "srv1" as NonEmptyStr,
    provision_parent_root: "/opt/gunbc" as FilePath,
    instance_root: "/opt/gunbc" as FilePath,
    local_origin_root: srv1_gunbc_repo_root,
    local_origin_publish_ref: "refs/heads/main" as NonEmptyStr,
    repo_root: srv1_gunbc_repo_root,
    dispatch_base_ref: "origin/main" as NonEmptyStr,
    publication_base_branch: "main" as NonEmptyStr,
    dispatch_state_scope: DispatchStateHostShared {
      worktree_root: srv1_dispatch_worktree_root,
      attempt_state_root: srv1_dispatch_attempt_state_root,
    },
    execution_environment: dashboard_execution_environment(
      serve_binary: srv1_gunbc_serve_binary,
    ),
    provider_state: DashboardExistingProviderState {
      root: srv1_roadmap_codex_home,
      executable: srv1_codex_bundled_executable(),
      binding: admitted_single_session_binding(
        selection: codex_roadmap_account_selection(),
        evidence: codex_roadmap_account_evidence,
      ),
    },
    serve_binary: srv1_gunbc_serve_binary,
    serve_function: "roadmap_serve_handle" as NonEmptyStr,
    listen_host: "127.0.0.1" as NonEmptyStr,
    listen_port: 8080,
    tmux_server: DefaultTmuxServer,
    actuation: DashboardActuating,
    provision_transport: DashboardSshProvision { host: "srv1" as NonEmptyStr },
    source_seed: DashboardExistingRepo { anchor_ref: "origin/main" as NonEmptyStr },
    admitted_revision: fleet_admitted_revision_source,
    toolchain: srv1_linux_toolchain,
    dispatch_worker_memory_max: dispatch_worker_memory_ceiling,
    directory_owner: linux_service_directory_owner(),
    worker: WorkerIsServiceUser,
    materialization_store_root: Present { value: DurableHostVolume },
  }
}

// host_identity USED TO BE A PARAMETER OVER A BODY OF srv1 LITERALS, which made a second host
// expressible without making it representable. Serve binary, provider state root, and source-seed
// controller repo were pinned to srv1 rows here, so srv2_preview_dashboard_instance below produced
// a value reading host_identity srv2 while describing srv1's binary, srv1's Codex home, and srv1's
// controller worktree. Nothing caught it because every consumer that would disagree — preflight,
// apply, the git and tmux observations — needs the host to actually be actuated, and that instance
// was declared observe-only and never provisioned.\n\nTaking the whole layout row instead of a bare
// host_identity makes the mismatch unwritable: there is no longer a way to name a host without also
// supplying the three facts that belong to it, because the name IS a field of the row that carries
// them. gunbc.host.host_layout owns those rows.

// EACH INSTANCE OWNS ITS BINARY, BECAUSE THE PROCESSES ARE MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE (operator ruling,
// 2026-07-30). The serve binary used to come from the host layout, so every isolated instance on
// one machine resolved to the same /opt/gunbc/bin/gunbc that production declares.
// dashboard_instance_owned_paths does not cover serve_binary — it checks origin, source, attempts
// and attempt-state — so the existing disjointness check passed while the DEPLOYMENT's
// owned-artifact set overlapped production on exactly that path.\n\nThe consequence was not a stale
// read. ServeBinary is an Owned artifact and retract tears Owned members down, so retracting a lab
// would have deleted the binary production is executing. That could not fire before deployments
// became expressible per instance, which is why it surfaced the moment the twin witness was written
// and not one commit earlier: the hazard is created by the capability, so it has to be closed in
// the same change that grants it.\n\nDeriving from the instance root rather than the layout also
// states the isolation the labs exist for — a lab testing a new seed must not overwrite the binary
// production is running. srv1-live is untouched: it declares /opt/gunbc/bin/gunbc directly rather
// than through this constructor, which is why its emitted apply and retract scripts stay
// byte-identical. GunbcHostLayout.gunbc_serve_binary keeps only srv1-live's own declaration as a
// consumer; it is a per-INSTANCE fact wearing a per-host name, and it dissolves when srv1-live is
// expressed as an instance row like the rest.

// WHICH REF A LAB SERVES IS THE LAB'S OWN FACT, and it was the fourth srv1-preview literal hiding
// in this constructor — found by probing the served endpoints after the other three were lifted,
// not by reading the diff. Every isolated instance inherited anchor_ref
// refs/heads/session/codex-provider-feedback-v0, so a newly added lab would have provisioned itself
// from a stale feedback branch belonging to a different lane while its page, its instance id, and
// its posture all read correctly. Nothing would have refused; it would simply have served the wrong
// source.\n\nThat is worse than the host-fact defect above, which produced identical values and no
// wrong output. This one produces a plausible page from unintended content — the failure that looks
// like success. It is a parameter rather than a layout field because two labs on ONE host may
// legitimately serve different refs; the ref belongs to the instance, not the machine.

fn isolated_dashboard_instance(
  deployment: DeploymentId,
  instance_id: NonEmptyStr,
  layout: GunbcHostLayout,
  root: FilePath,
  serve_function: NonEmptyStr,
  listen_port: Port,
  source_anchor_ref: NonEmptyStr,
  actuation: DashboardActuationPosture,
  provision_transport: DashboardProvisionTransport,
) -> HostDashboardInstance {
  isolated_dashboard_instance_admitting(
    deployment: deployment, instance_id: instance_id, layout: layout, root: root,
    serve_function: serve_function, listen_port: listen_port, source_anchor_ref: source_anchor_ref,
    actuation: actuation, provision_transport: provision_transport,
    admitted_revision: fleet_admitted_revision_source,
    publication_base_branch: "main" as NonEmptyStr,
    worker: WorkerIsServiceUser,
    provision_parent_root: dashboard_home_provision_parent_root,
  )
}

// An isolated instance whose admitted revision is NOT the fleet's: a development instance that serves
// a branch is admitted by a ref in its own repository the operator advances deliberately (the
// DashboardAdmittedRevisionSource annotation; precedent srv2_deploy_admitted_revision_source).
fn isolated_dashboard_instance_admitting(
  deployment: DeploymentId,
  instance_id: NonEmptyStr,
  layout: GunbcHostLayout,
  root: FilePath,
  serve_function: NonEmptyStr,
  listen_port: Port,
  source_anchor_ref: NonEmptyStr,
  actuation: DashboardActuationPosture,
  provision_transport: DashboardProvisionTransport,
  admitted_revision: DashboardAdmittedRevisionSource,
  publication_base_branch: NonEmptyStr,
  worker: WorkerIdentity,
  provision_parent_root: FilePath,
) -> HostDashboardInstance {
  HostDashboardInstance {
    deployment: deployment,
    instance_id: instance_id,
    host_identity: layout.host_identity,
    provision_parent_root: provision_parent_root,
    instance_root: root,
    local_origin_root: dashboard_instance_child(root: root, child: "origin.git"),
    local_origin_publish_ref: "refs/heads/main" as NonEmptyStr,
    repo_root: dashboard_instance_child(root: root, child: "source"),
    dispatch_base_ref: "origin/main" as NonEmptyStr,
    publication_base_branch: publication_base_branch,
    dispatch_state_scope: DispatchStateInstanceOwned {
      worktree_child: "attempts" as NonEmptyStr,
      attempt_state_child: "attempt-state" as NonEmptyStr,
    },
    execution_environment: dashboard_execution_environment(
      serve_binary: dashboard_instance_child(root: root, child: "bin/gunbc"),
    ),
    provider_state: DashboardExistingProviderState {
      root: layout.roadmap_codex_home,
      executable: layout.codex_bundled_executable,
      binding: admitted_single_session_binding(
        selection: codex_roadmap_account_selection(),
        evidence: codex_roadmap_account_evidence,
      ),
    },
    serve_binary: dashboard_instance_child(root: root, child: "bin/gunbc"),
    serve_function: serve_function,
    listen_host: "0.0.0.0" as NonEmptyStr,
    listen_port: listen_port,
    tmux_server: dashboard_named_tmux_server(instance_id: instance_id),
    actuation: actuation,
    provision_transport: provision_transport,
    source_seed: DashboardGitBundle {
      controller_repo: layout.controller_repo,
      staged_bundle: dashboard_instance_child(root: dashboard_inbox_under(root: root), child: "source.bundle"),
      anchor_ref: source_anchor_ref,
      history_remote: "origin" as NonEmptyStr,
    },
    admitted_revision: admitted_revision,
    toolchain: srv1_linux_toolchain,
    dispatch_worker_memory_max: dispatch_worker_memory_ceiling,
    directory_owner: linux_service_directory_owner(),
    worker: worker,
    materialization_store_root: none,
  }
}

// THE SRV1 LAB SERVES A DEVELOPMENT BRANCH, SO MAIN CANNOT BE ITS ADMITTING AUTHORITY. Under the fleet's
// origin/main every landing on main refused all lab dispatch ('deployed_revision_unobserved': the lab's
// repository did not even hold main's new head) until the next redeploy (srv1 lab, 2026-10-11, twice
// in one night). The operator admits the lab's tree by advancing this ref -- a visible act with a
// reflog receipt -- exactly as srv2-deploy does.
data srv1_lab_admitted_revision_source: DashboardAdmittedRevisionSource = DashboardAdmittedRevisionSource {
  remote: ".",
  ref_name: "refs/gunbc/srv1-lab-admitted" as GitRef,
}

fn srv1_lab_dashboard_instance() -> HostDashboardInstance {
  isolated_dashboard_instance_admitting(
    deployment: srv1_lab_codex_feedback_deployment,
    instance_id: srv1_dashboard_lab_id,
    layout: srv1_host_layout(),
    root: srv1_dashboard_lab_root,
    serve_function: "roadmap_serve_handle_srv1_lab" as NonEmptyStr,
    listen_port: 18081,
    source_anchor_ref: "refs/remotes/origin/main" as NonEmptyStr,
    actuation: DashboardActuating,
    provision_transport: DashboardSshProvision { host: "srv1" as NonEmptyStr },
    admitted_revision: srv1_lab_admitted_revision_source,
    publication_base_branch: srv1_lab_publication_base_branch,
    worker: WorkerDedicatedAccount,
    provision_parent_root: srv1_dashboard_lab_provision_parent,
  )
}

fn srv2_preview_dashboard_instance() -> HostDashboardInstance {
  isolated_dashboard_instance(
    deployment: srv2_preview_codex_feedback_deployment,
    instance_id: srv2_dashboard_preview_id,
    layout: srv2_host_layout(),
    root: srv2_dashboard_preview_root,
    serve_function: "roadmap_serve_handle_srv2_preview" as NonEmptyStr,
    listen_port: 18080,
    source_anchor_ref: "refs/heads/session/codex-provider-feedback-v0" as NonEmptyStr,
    actuation: DashboardObserveOnly,
    provision_transport: DashboardLocalProvision,
  )
}

// THE ACTUATING srv2 INSTANCE, added because the preview one could never have worked and its
// failure mode was silent until someone ran it. srv2_preview above is DashboardObserveOnly, so its
// dispatch endpoint refuses by construction — correct, and invisible on a page whose buttons simply
// read disabled. It also had no entry in gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_dashboard_instance_apply, which contained
// zero srv2 references, so nothing in the tree could provision the directories its observations
// refuse against. Declared, servable, and unreachable in the same value.\n\nThis row is the
// actuating peer: same isolated layout, its own root and named tmux server, DashboardActuating so
// dispatch is admitted, DashboardLocalProvision because srv2 provisions itself rather than over
// SSH. A port collision resolved by passing a different --port on the command line is how the last
// deployment ended up serving one instance's page under another instance's identity.\n\nThe preview
// row is deliberately kept rather than repointed. It is the observe-only posture's only live
// specimen, several witnesses discriminate against it, and deleting a row because a better one
// arrived is how the 2026-07-27 authority refresh lost the only execution contract it had.

data srv2_dashboard_lab_id: NonEmptyStr = "srv2-lab-v1-deletion" as NonEmptyStr

data srv2_dashboard_lab_root: FilePath = "/home/briansrls/.local/share/gunbc-dashboard-instances/srv2-lab-v1-deletion" as FilePath

// THE srv2 DEPLOYMENT INSTANCE, rooted at a DEDICATED CLONE rather than at a lab's provisioned
// source or at an operator's working checkout. Both alternatives were tried and each fails for its
// own reason, which is why this row exists instead of a fourth parameter on one of them.
//
// srv2_lab_dashboard_instance below is fed from its own local origin.git bundle, and that bundle
// advertises no origin/main -- so every dispatch against it refuses with
// deployed_revision_unobserved. The refusal is CORRECT: the lab is isolated by construction and its
// origin is not the fleet's. Pushing main into that bundle by hand would be out-of-band
// actuation against provisioned state a modeled apply owns, and it would not help anyway, because
// the lab's source is pinned to the ref it was seeded from and attempts would branch off that.
//
// Serving from a development checkout is the opposite failure: dispatch creates worktrees from
// repo_root, so an actuating instance rooted at a tree someone is editing would branch attempts off
// uncommitted work and race the editor. The clone is therefore the SUBJECT of the deployment while
// the checkout stays the place work is authored -- the same tool/subject separation
// gunbc_harness_spawn_argv already draws for the worker.
//
// The admitting ref follows macbook_local's shape rather than the fleet's, and for the same reason
// stated there: a deployment's admitted revision is not a fact origin has any business advertising.
// Advancing it is `git update-ref refs/gunbc/srv2-admitted HEAD` inside the clone, and the friction
// is the feature -- the gate keeps its exact form and only the NAMED REF varies, so this is one
// binding of one operation rather than an exemption.
data srv2_deploy_dashboard_id: NonEmptyStr = "srv2-deploy" as NonEmptyStr

data srv2_deploy_repo_root: FilePath = "/home/briansrls/gunbc-srv2-deploy" as FilePath

data srv2_deploy_instance_root: FilePath = materialization_store_persistent_srv2_scope as FilePath

data srv2_deploy_serve_binary: FilePath = join([cargo_default_release_dir(build_root: srv2_deploy_repo_root as String), "/gunbc"], "") as FilePath

// THE BRANCH ATTEMPTS ARE PUBLISHED AGAINST IS THE BRANCH THEIR BASE REVISION BELONGS TO, declared per
// instance (publication_base_branch) rather than derived. Reading the repository's default_branch
// offered a srv2 attempt (dispatched from origin/srv2-deploy) against main with sixty unrelated commits
// (2026-09-05); deriving it by stripping "origin/" from dispatch_base_ref then broke the same way for
// the srv1 lab, whose "origin/main" is rewritten to its admitted lab-dev revision
// (srv1_lab_publication_base_branch). The branch is a fact about where the base revision lives on the
// publication remote, so each instance states it.
fn dispatch_base_branch(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> NonEmptyStr {
  instance.publication_base_branch
}

// THE SRV1 LAB PUBLISHES INTO ITS INTEGRATION BRANCH (operator, 2026-10-13). Its attempts fork from the
// lab's admitted revision: the lab deploy rewrites the source repository's refs/remotes/origin/main to
// that revision (reflog, 2026-10-12: 5935eae at 03:04, cf420d9 at 17:08), so the row's "origin/main"
// names the admitted lab-dev commit, not GitHub main. Deriving the publication base from that name
// offered lab attempts against GitHub main, with every lab-only commit in the diff. The admitted
// revision is a commit of this branch, so a pull request against it carries only the worker's change.
data srv1_lab_publication_base_branch: NonEmptyStr = "bright-moth-475/lab-dev" as NonEmptyStr

data srv2_deploy_admitted_revision_source: DashboardAdmittedRevisionSource = DashboardAdmittedRevisionSource {
  remote: ".",
  ref_name: "refs/gunbc/srv2-admitted" as GitRef,
}

// THE SRV2 DEPLOY INSTANCE BRANCHES ATTEMPTS FROM origin/main AGAIN. While its deploy branch was ahead
// of main it branched from origin/srv2-deploy, because an attempt based on main would have lacked every
// oracle its node named; that branch landed (PR gunb-ai/gunbc#10443, 2026-09-05) and the row returned
// here as its own annotation said it would. The clone's origin is GitHub, so publication runs here and
// attempts are published against main (dispatch_base_branch); the admitted revision stays the local ref.
fn srv2_deploy_dashboard_instance() -> HostDashboardInstance {
  HostDashboardInstance {
    deployment: srv2_deploy_deployment,
    instance_id: srv2_deploy_dashboard_id,
    host_identity: srv2_host_layout().host_identity,
    provision_parent_root: dashboard_home_provision_parent_root,
    instance_root: srv2_deploy_instance_root,
    local_origin_root: srv2_deploy_repo_root,
    local_origin_publish_ref: "refs/heads/main" as NonEmptyStr,
    repo_root: srv2_deploy_repo_root,
    dispatch_base_ref: "test/main-plus-roadmap-daily" as NonEmptyStr,
    publication_base_branch: "test/main-plus-roadmap-daily" as NonEmptyStr,
    dispatch_state_scope: DispatchStateInstanceOwned {
      worktree_child: "attempts" as NonEmptyStr,
      attempt_state_child: "attempt-state" as NonEmptyStr,
    },
    execution_environment: dashboard_execution_environment_for_toolchain(
      toolchain: srv1_linux_toolchain,
      serve_binary: srv2_deploy_serve_binary,
    ),
    provider_state: DashboardExistingProviderState {
      root: srv2_host_layout().roadmap_codex_home,
      executable: srv2_host_layout().codex_bundled_executable,
      binding: admitted_single_session_binding(
        selection: codex_roadmap_account_selection(),
        evidence: codex_roadmap_account_evidence,
      ),
    },
    serve_binary: srv2_deploy_serve_binary,
    serve_function: "roadmap_serve_handle_srv2_deploy" as NonEmptyStr,
    listen_host: "0.0.0.0" as NonEmptyStr,
    listen_port: 18082,
    tmux_server: dashboard_named_tmux_server(instance_id: srv2_deploy_dashboard_id),
    actuation: DashboardActuating,
    provision_transport: DashboardLocalProvision,
    source_seed: DashboardExistingRepo { anchor_ref: "origin/main" as NonEmptyStr },
    admitted_revision: srv2_deploy_admitted_revision_source,
    toolchain: srv1_linux_toolchain,
    dispatch_worker_memory_max: dispatch_worker_memory_ceiling,
    directory_owner: linux_service_directory_owner(),
    worker: WorkerIsServiceUser,
    materialization_store_root: Present { value: PersistentInstanceWorkspace { scope: Srv2Deploy } },
  }
}

// Operator statement, 2026-09-11 (relayed by bright-eagle-728): "srv2:18082 is like a dev
// instance, i'm not sure who/what is using it". The operator directed this lab to 18083,
// unused by the declared roster, leaving srv2-deploy at 18082. This resolves the declaration
// collision; it is not an ownership determination or an observation of either live listener.
fn srv2_lab_dashboard_instance() -> HostDashboardInstance {
  isolated_dashboard_instance(
    deployment: srv2_lab_v1_deletion_deployment,
    instance_id: srv2_dashboard_lab_id,
    layout: srv2_host_layout(),
    root: srv2_dashboard_lab_root,
    serve_function: "roadmap_serve_handle_srv2_lab" as NonEmptyStr,
    listen_port: 18083,
    source_anchor_ref: "refs/remotes/origin/main" as NonEmptyStr,
    actuation: DashboardActuating,
    provision_transport: DashboardLocalProvision,
  )
}

// THE PUBLIC BUILT-IN SET, and the name says PUBLIC BUILT-IN because the set is not "every
// instance capable of consuming this machinery". It is the instances THIS REPOSITORY declares.
// A deployment declared in another repository consumes the same generic folds and is deliberately
// absent here: listing it would require the public repo to name a private one.
//
// WHAT FOLDS THIS, AND WHAT DOES NOT. The three lookups fold it to RESOLVE an instance from a host,
// a repo root or an id. Resolution is not admission: no fold checks membership before acting, so a
// generic effectful fold that RECEIVES an instance serves it whether or not it is listed here, and
// that is the property a caller-supplied instance depends on. The safety obligation membership used
// to carry travels separately, as dashboard_instance_is_disjoint_from_public_builtins, which an
// outside caller applies to itself against this set.
//
// Isolated labs belong here because they have repo roots a belt tick can land in — omitting them
// made those roots DashboardInstanceRootUnknown while the constructors existed, which is the same
// fork as enumerating production three times.
// THE FLEET HERMETIC COMPUTE SUBJECT (WAVE-0 B). A declared instance the compute_run_unit
// instrument binds, not a session cwd and not srv1-live's /opt/gunbc/gunbc. host_identity is
// ghrunner-hermetic so srv1-live keeps host lookup. Source (repo_root, local_origin_root) is the
// invoking job workspace; every write root is the frame's scratch root,
// materialization_store_frame_root(frame), never a durable host path (/opt/gunbc). Observe-only:
// this row names a compute layout, it does not grant dispatch. Not a public_builtin: a
// frame-scoped root is not a host lookup subject.
//
// TOOLCHAIN IS A PARAMETER, the same way workspace is. srv1_linux_toolchain names a developer
// home (`/home/briansrls/.cargo/bin/cargo`); a fleet job isolates CARGO_HOME and RUSTUP_HOME
// under RUNNER_TEMP and refuses ToolchainHomesNotIsolated otherwise. Cargo/rustc/rustfmt are
// the shims under cargo_home/bin; they dispatch through rustup_home. Neither PATH nor a
// literal developer path is a constructor.
data fleet_compute_hermetic_dashboard_id: NonEmptyStr = "fleet-compute-hermetic" as NonEmptyStr
data fleet_compute_hermetic_directory_owner: PosixOwnerNames = PosixOwnerNames {
  user: "ghrunner" as NonEmptyStr,
  group: "ghrunner" as NonEmptyStr,
}

type IsolatedJobToolchainHomes {
  cargo_home: FilePath
  rustup_home: FilePath
}

fn fleet_compute_hermetic_rust_shim(cargo_home: FilePath, bin: String) -> FilePath {
  dashboard_instance_child(root: cargo_home, child: join(["bin/", bin], ""))
}

fn fleet_compute_hermetic_toolchain(homes: IsolatedJobToolchainHomes) -> DashboardToolchain {
  DashboardToolchain {
    directory_create: "/usr/bin/mkdir" as FilePath,
    path_test: "/usr/bin/test" as FilePath,
    path_stat: "/usr/bin/stat" as FilePath,
    path_list: "/usr/bin/ls" as FilePath,
    git: "/usr/bin/git" as FilePath,
    tmux: "/usr/bin/tmux" as FilePath,
    pipe_shell: "/usr/bin/sh" as FilePath,
    tee: "/usr/bin/tee" as FilePath,
    jq: "/usr/bin/jq" as FilePath,
    environment_binding: dashboard_environment_binding_executable,
    install: "/usr/bin/install" as FilePath,
    link: "/usr/bin/ln" as FilePath,
    timeout: "/usr/bin/timeout" as FilePath,
    systemd_run: Present { value: "/usr/bin/systemd-run" as FilePath },
    cargo: fleet_compute_hermetic_rust_shim(cargo_home: homes.cargo_home, bin: "cargo"),
    rustc: fleet_compute_hermetic_rust_shim(cargo_home: homes.cargo_home, bin: "rustc"),
    rustfmt: fleet_compute_hermetic_rust_shim(cargo_home: homes.cargo_home, bin: "rustfmt"),
  }
}

// THE HERMETIC INSTANCE OWNS ONLY ITS FRAME. Its source is the job's workspace (repo_root and
// origin), and everything it writes -- compute root, store, staging -- lives under
// /tmp/gunbc_compute_<frame>, the frame-owned scratch grant, so a self-hosted slot's persistent
// workspace never holds a store typed EphemeralToFrame.
fn fleet_compute_hermetic_dashboard_instance_at(workspace: FilePath, frame: Int, toolchain_homes: IsolatedJobToolchainHomes) -> HostDashboardInstance {
  let instance_root = materialization_store_frame_root(frame: frame) as FilePath
  let toolchain = fleet_compute_hermetic_toolchain(homes: toolchain_homes)
  HostDashboardInstance {
    deployment: fleet_compute_hermetic_deployment,
    instance_id: fleet_compute_hermetic_dashboard_id,
    host_identity: "ghrunner-hermetic" as NonEmptyStr,
    provision_parent_root: instance_root,
    instance_root: instance_root,
    local_origin_root: workspace,
    local_origin_publish_ref: "refs/heads/main" as NonEmptyStr,
    repo_root: workspace,
    dispatch_base_ref: "origin/main" as NonEmptyStr,
    publication_base_branch: "main" as NonEmptyStr,
    dispatch_state_scope: DispatchStateInstanceOwned {
      worktree_child: "attempts" as NonEmptyStr,
      attempt_state_child: "attempt-state" as NonEmptyStr,
    },
    execution_environment: dashboard_execution_environment_for_toolchain(
      toolchain: toolchain,
      serve_binary: dashboard_instance_child(root: instance_root, child: "bin/gunbc"),
    ),
    provider_state: DashboardExistingProviderState {
      root: dashboard_instance_child(root: instance_root, child: "codex"),
      executable: none,
      binding: admitted_single_session_binding(
        selection: CodexDefaultAccount,
        evidence: codex_roadmap_account_evidence,
      ),
    },
    serve_binary: dashboard_instance_child(root: instance_root, child: "bin/gunbc"),
    serve_function: "roadmap_serve_handle" as NonEmptyStr,
    listen_host: "127.0.0.1" as NonEmptyStr,
    listen_port: 18084,
    tmux_server: dashboard_named_tmux_server(instance_id: fleet_compute_hermetic_dashboard_id),
    actuation: DashboardObserveOnly,
    provision_transport: DashboardLocalProvision,
    source_seed: DashboardExistingRepo { anchor_ref: "origin/main" as NonEmptyStr },
    admitted_revision: fleet_admitted_revision_source,
    toolchain: toolchain,
    dispatch_worker_memory_max: dispatch_worker_memory_ceiling,
    directory_owner: fleet_compute_hermetic_directory_owner,
    worker: WorkerIsServiceUser,
    materialization_store_root: Present { value: FrameJobWorkspace { frame: frame } },
  }
}

fn public_builtin_dashboard_instances() -> List<HostDashboardInstance> {
  [
    srv1_live_dashboard_instance(),
    srv1_lab_dashboard_instance(),
    srv2_preview_dashboard_instance(),
    srv2_lab_dashboard_instance(),
    srv2_deploy_dashboard_instance(),
    macbook_local_dashboard_instance(),
  ]
}

fn dashboard_declared_instance_ids_are_unique() -> Bool {
  let xs = public_builtin_dashboard_instances()
  all(xs, a => count(xs |> filter(b => b.instance_id == a.instance_id)) == 1)
}

fn dashboard_declared_repo_roots_are_unique() -> Bool {
  let xs = public_builtin_dashboard_instances()
  all(xs, a => count(xs |> filter(b => b.repo_root == a.repo_root)) == 1)
}

fn dashboard_declared_instance_roots_are_unique() -> Bool {
  let xs = public_builtin_dashboard_instances()
  all(xs, a => count(xs |> filter(b => b.instance_root == a.instance_root)) == 1)
}

// The uniqueness law above compares spellings, so it means "one directory per instance" only over roots
// spelled one way; this is that precondition, over any instance set, and read off the same declared rows.
fn dashboard_instance_roots_are_one_spelling(instances: List<HostDashboardInstance>) -> Bool {
  all(instances, i => match dashboard_instance_root_spelling_refusal(instance: i) {
    Present { value: _ } => false
    Absent => true
  })
}

fn dashboard_declared_instance_roots_are_one_spelling() -> Bool {
  dashboard_instance_roots_are_one_spelling(instances: public_builtin_dashboard_instances())
}

// AT MOST ONE per host_identity among instances that answer host lookup. Distinct instance_ids
// do not discharge this: two deployments on srv1 can have different ids and still both claim
// the hostname.
fn dashboard_host_lookup_subjects_are_unique() -> Bool {
  let xs = public_builtin_dashboard_instances()
    |> filter(i => dashboard_instance_answers_host_lookup(instance: i))
  all(xs, a =>
    count(xs |> filter(b => b.host_identity == a.host_identity)) == 1
  )
}

// ONE CANDIDATE AGAINST A SET, which is the shape BOTH callers need and the reason it is factored
// out rather than written twice. The public law below quantifies it over its own roster; a caller
// holding an instance that is NOT in that roster -- a deployment declared in another repository --
// asks the same question against the same judgment instead of coining a second disjointness rule.
//
// NO IDENTITY SKIP HERE. A caller-chosen instance_id is not record identity: the public uniqueness
// law only quantifies over the roster, so an outside deployment can spell "srv1-live" and, if the
// skip were on that tag, be treated as "itself" against the production row while sitting on that
// row's owned paths. HostDashboardInstance is not an equality-admissible record (it carries
// function-bearing environment and capability members), so the skip cannot be `candidate == b`
// either. The diagonal lives only in the roster law, which filters by instance_id among members
// whose uniqueness is a separate conjunct. This fold always asks the owned-path judgment.
fn dashboard_instance_is_disjoint_from_each(
  candidate: HostDashboardInstance,
  others: List<HostDashboardInstance>,
) -> Bool {
  all(
    others,
    b => dashboard_instances_have_disjoint_owned_paths(a: candidate, b: b),
  )
}

// THE CROSS-REPOSITORY ENTRY. An instance declared outside this repository cannot be a member of
// the roster below -- membership would require the public repo to name a private one, reversing a
// dependency direction that is not negotiable -- so admission cannot be membership. It is this
// join: the caller's own instance against the public built-ins, on the public judgment.
fn dashboard_instance_is_disjoint_from_public_builtins(
  candidate: HostDashboardInstance,
) -> Bool {
  dashboard_instance_is_disjoint_from_each(
    candidate: candidate,
    others: public_builtin_dashboard_instances(),
  )
}

// THE DISJOINTNESS LAW OVER THE SET, not over the pairs a witness remembered. The pairwise
// predicate is the judgment and dashboard_instance_is_disjoint_from_each is one candidate against
// the set; this is the quantification over the roster. The diagonal is THIS filter: instance_id
// among members whose uniqueness is a separate law, not a skip inside the join an outside
// caller uses. A new roster row is in the cross product the moment it is declared, so a lab
// that overlaps production cannot sit green until someone writes a second call.
fn dashboard_declared_instances_owned_paths_are_pairwise_consistent() -> Bool {
  let xs = public_builtin_dashboard_instances()
  all(
    xs,
    a =>
      dashboard_instance_is_disjoint_from_each(
        candidate: a,
        others: xs |> filter(b => b.instance_id != a.instance_id),
      ),
  )
}

// Endpoint ownership is independent of instance id and owned paths. Count the diagonal
// exactly once rather than exempting rows by a caller-authored id. This checks declared
// endpoints; it cannot establish which process is currently listening on the host.
fn dashboard_instances_listen_endpoints_are_unique(
  instances: List<HostDashboardInstance>,
) -> Bool {
  all(
    instances,
    a => count(instances |> filter(b =>
      b.host_identity == a.host_identity && b.listen_port == a.listen_port
    )) == 1,
  )
}

fn dashboard_declared_instances_listen_endpoints_are_unique() -> Bool {
  dashboard_instances_listen_endpoints_are_unique(
    instances: public_builtin_dashboard_instances(),
  )
}

fn dashboard_path_is_under(path: FilePath, root: FilePath) -> Bool {
  starts_with(s: path as String, prefix: concat(root as String, "/"))
}

// serve_binary WAS MISSING FROM THE CANONICAL OWNED SET while live_deploy treated it as an Owned
// artifact and removed it on retract — two models of one fact, one saying owned-and-retractable and
// the other saying neither (review, 2026-07-30). The omission was correct while instances BORROWED
// production's binary: a path you do not own does not belong in your owned set. Making it
// instance-derived changed the answer and nothing propagated it here.\n\nThe consequence was
// concrete rather than tidy. dashboard_instances_have_disjoint_owned_paths reads this roster, so
// the disjointness check could not see the one path two instances actually shared — which is why
// the twin witness had to assert binary inequality as a SEPARATE conjunct rather than getting it
// from the canonical set. A check that needs a hand-written supplement to cover the thing it exists
// to cover is not covering it.\n\nIt belongs here for both instance shapes: srv1-live's
// /opt/gunbc/bin/gunbc sits under its instance_root /opt/gunbc, and an isolated lab's sits under
// its own root by derivation, so dashboard_instance_paths_are_owned's containment law holds for
// both without exception. The twin claim can now drop its supplemental conjunct and read the
// roster.

// ONE ROSTER USED TO CARRY TWO KINDS OF MEMBER, and the disjointness law could not be right about
// both once production slots began sharing dispatch state. The roster mixed paths that are the
// instance's own FOOTPRINT — its origin, its source tree, its binary — with paths that hold
// DISPATCH EVIDENCE, and then applied a single rule to the union: every path sits under
// instance_root, and no two instances share any path.\n\nThat rule is exactly right for the
// footprint and exactly wrong for host-shared dispatch state, whose whole purpose is to be the same
// path in two slots. Weakening the law to admit overlap would have been the cheap fix and the wrong
// one: overlap is precisely the hazard owned_paths_omitted_the_binary_note records, where a lab's
// retract would have deleted the binary production was executing. So the roster splits and each
// half keeps the law that is true of it, rather than one roster keeping a law that is true of
// neither.\n\nFootprint paths keep BOTH original laws at full strength, for every instance and
// every scope. Dispatch-state paths get the law their scope declares: instance-owned must sit under
// instance_root and stay disjoint from every other instance, which is the old behaviour preserved
// for labs; host-shared must EQUAL the layout rows and therefore agree with every other host-shared
// instance on the same host. Agreement is a stronger statement than the absence of a check — it
// goes red if a production slot is given its own attempt-state root, which is the regression this
// change exists to prevent recurring.

fn dashboard_instance_footprint_paths(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> List<FilePath> {
  [
    instance.local_origin_root,
    instance.repo_root,
    instance.serve_binary,
  ]
}

// ── THE DIRECTORIES THIS MODULE'S OWN CONSUMERS DEMAND ──────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Each is an owned-directory target (gunbc.owned_directory) built from the instance, and the path every
// runtime reader uses is read OFF the target, so the directory that is ensured and the directory that
// is written cannot name two paths. The other demands live with their consumers -- the auth stores,
// the view-preference store, the event carrier, the compute provider, the fabric store -- and
// gunbc.roadmap.dashboard_instance_directories dashboard_instance_directory_demands composes them.
//
// The worktree root keeps the shared 0755 it was installed at: dispatched workers run as the service
// principal, and no reader of it has been established that would justify tightening a live directory.
// The two dispatch roots' member names, named once: a live_deploy retract reads them to keep the
// empty-directory refusal that guards the source tree (gunbc.live_deploy.retract_legs retract_dispatch_root_paths).
data dashboard_dispatch_worktree_member: NonEmptyStr = "dispatch-worktree-root" as NonEmptyStr

// THE WORKER'S IDENTITY IS A DECLARED TWO-ARM SUM, NOT A COMPARISON OF USER NAMES. WorkerIsServiceUser is the
// STAGED form of a replacement migration (DESIGN section 3, gap-intolerant boundary): the worker runs as
// the instance's service user (X). WorkerDedicatedAccount is Y: gunbc.fleet.fleet_posix_accounts
// fleet_posix_worker_user in the shared attempt group, both read from that row, so no instance can name a
// third account or another group. Y runs in shadow on the srv1 lab; the cutover (an operator decision
// after the lab qualifies) deletes the first arm and this sum with it.
type WorkerIdentity
  = WorkerIsServiceUser
  | WorkerDedicatedAccount

// The account the worker unit runs as, derived from the arm.
fn dashboard_instance_worker_user(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match instance.worker {
    WorkerIsServiceUser => instance.directory_owner.user
    WorkerDedicatedAccount => fleet_posix_worker_user.name
  }
}

// OWNER OF A DIRECTORY THE WORKER WRITES: the service user keeps ownership; a dedicated worker's group is the
// shared attempt group. The staged arm keeps the pre-existing pair, so prod's directories are unchanged.
fn dashboard_instance_worker_writable_owner(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> PosixOwnerNames {
  match instance.worker {
    WorkerIsServiceUser => instance.directory_owner
    WorkerDedicatedAccount => PosixOwnerNames { user: instance.directory_owner.user, group: fleet_posix_worker_shared_group_name }
  }
}

fn dashboard_instance_worker_writable_mode(instance: HostDashboardInstance, staged: DeclaredDirectoryMode) -> DeclaredDirectoryMode {
  match instance.worker {
    WorkerIsServiceUser => staged
    WorkerDedicatedAccount => DirectoryOwnerGroupWritableSetgid
  }
}

// THE GROUP-REGROUP POSTURE FOR A DIRECTORY THE WORKER WRITES, declared beside the owner just above so
// the fact that creates the mismatch and the posture that repairs it are read together. The SAME
// `instance.worker` discriminator that groups these directories to the shared attempt group (which is
// exactly why a pre-existing briansrls:briansrls directory fails the historical both-match test) is the
// one that authorizes re-grouping it back: for a dedicated worker a directory the service user already
// owns may be re-grouped to the target's declared group, and for the staged arm (prod, WorkerIsServiceUser)
// the posture is ForeignOwnershipRefuses -- the historical "ownership is never adopted", so prod is
// untouched (WorkerIsServiceUser). The group it may move to is always the target's OWN declared group
// (owned_directory_owner(t).group, named by the install), never an observed one.
fn dashboard_instance_worker_writable_group_adoption(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> OwnedDirectoryGroupAdoption {
  match instance.worker {
    WorkerIsServiceUser => ForeignOwnershipRefuses
    WorkerDedicatedAccount => SameUserRegroupToDeclaredGroup
  }
}

data dashboard_attempt_state_member: NonEmptyStr = "attempt-state-root" as NonEmptyStr

fn dashboard_instance_dispatch_worktree_directory(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> OwnedDirectoryTarget {
  OwnedDirectoryDeclared {
    member: dashboard_dispatch_worktree_member,
    path: match instance.dispatch_state_scope {
      DispatchStateInstanceOwned { worktree_child, attempt_state_child: _ } =>
        dashboard_instance_child(root: instance.instance_root, child: worktree_child)
      DispatchStateHostShared { worktree_root, attempt_state_root: _ } => worktree_root
    },
    owner: dashboard_instance_worker_writable_owner(instance: instance),
    mode: dashboard_instance_worker_writable_mode(instance: instance, staged: DirectoryOwnerAllEveryoneTraverse),
    group_adoption: dashboard_instance_worker_writable_group_adoption(instance: instance),
  }
}

fn dashboard_instance_dispatch_worktree_root(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> FilePath {
  owned_directory_path(t: dashboard_instance_dispatch_worktree_directory(instance: instance))
}

// AN AUTH-STATE DIRECTORY UNDER THE INSTANCE ROOT, the shape every auth store's demand shares: owned by
// the instance's principal and closed to everyone else (sessions, login transactions, the JWKS cache,
// profiles and view preferences are read and written only by the serve process). Each store declares
// its own demand with this shape (gunbc.auth.session_store, gunbc.auth.login_transaction_store, ...);
// the segment is the store's fact, not this module's.
fn dashboard_instance_private_directory(instance: HostDashboardInstance, member: NonEmptyStr, child: String) -> OwnedDirectoryTarget {
  OwnedDirectoryDeclared {
    member: member,
    path: dashboard_instance_child(root: instance.instance_root, child: child),
    owner: instance.directory_owner,
    mode: DirectoryOwnerOnly,
    group_adoption: ForeignOwnershipRefuses,
  }
}

// A DIRECTORY UNDER THE INSTANCE ROOT AT THE SHARED 0755 the provision plan's mkdir produced, for a
// consumer whose readers have not been established beyond the instance's principal.
fn dashboard_instance_shared_directory(instance: HostDashboardInstance, member: NonEmptyStr, child: String) -> OwnedDirectoryTarget {
  OwnedDirectoryDeclared {
    member: member,
    path: dashboard_instance_child(root: instance.instance_root, child: child),
    owner: instance.directory_owner,
    mode: DirectoryOwnerAllEveryoneTraverse,
    group_adoption: ForeignOwnershipRefuses,
  }
}

// The attempt-state root is 0700: the dependents gunbc.managed_directory attempt_state_directory_at
// states are the service principal alone, and that derivation is what this mode equals.
fn dashboard_instance_dispatch_attempt_state_directory(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> OwnedDirectoryTarget {
  OwnedDirectoryDeclared {
    member: dashboard_attempt_state_member,
    path: match instance.dispatch_state_scope {
      DispatchStateInstanceOwned { worktree_child: _, attempt_state_child } =>
        dashboard_instance_child(root: instance.instance_root, child: attempt_state_child)
      DispatchStateHostShared { worktree_root: _, attempt_state_root } => attempt_state_root
    },
    owner: dashboard_instance_worker_writable_owner(instance: instance),
    mode: dashboard_instance_worker_writable_mode(instance: instance, staged: DirectoryOwnerOnly),
    group_adoption: dashboard_instance_worker_writable_group_adoption(instance: instance),
  }
}

fn dashboard_instance_dispatch_attempt_state_root(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> FilePath {
  owned_directory_path(t: dashboard_instance_dispatch_attempt_state_directory(instance: instance))
}

// THE MODEL-ENDPOINT CREDENTIAL'S CUSTODY: the provider login writes the model credential under this
// root and the worker reads it, so for a dedicated worker the root is owned by the service user, grouped
// to the shared attempt group and group-writable (the worker's refresh writes there too). The deploy
// asserts this owner and mode through the owned-directory provider; nothing is chowned by hand.
//
// THE PROVIDER STATE ROOT is a directory, never a credential: the provider login writes into it, and
// nothing the deploy carries populates it. It lives outside the instance root (so it is never an owned
// footprint path) and nothing tears it down.
fn dashboard_instance_provider_state_directory(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> OwnedDirectoryTarget {
  OwnedDirectoryDeclared {
    member: "provider-state-root" as NonEmptyStr,
    path: dashboard_instance_provider_state_root(instance: instance),
    owner: dashboard_instance_worker_writable_owner(instance: instance),
    mode: dashboard_instance_worker_writable_mode(instance: instance, staged: DirectoryOwnerAllEveryoneTraverse),
    group_adoption: dashboard_instance_worker_writable_group_adoption(instance: instance),
  }
}

// THE GIT DIRECTORIES A DEDICATED WORKER WRITES (WORKER-ROOT-EQUIVALENT-2, item 2). The worker commits
// into repo_root/.git/objects, refs and logs (gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_dispatch_actuator
// dispatch_worker_git_writable_paths), which the service user owns; a unit running as gunbc-worker
// reaches them only through the shared attempt group, so each is declared group-writable setgid exactly as
// the worktree and attempt-state roots are. NOT declared: .git itself, hooks, config, info and the
// .git/worktrees parent, which stay service-user-only (gunbc#13942's carve-out). The staged arm declares
// none, so prod's repository is untouched.
// DECLARED RESIDUALS: the provider asserts these directories, not their existing contents (it does not
// recurse), and packed-refs plus the per-attempt .git/worktrees/<key> are a file and a runtime-created
// directory it cannot assert; both are read back by the lab qualification (a live worker commits).
// THE THREE SHARED SUBTREES, as children of the repo root, plus the per-attempt entry the dispatch
// actuator names (below, at WORKER-ROOT-EQUIVALENT-3, because this module has no business naming an
// attempt). `.git/worktrees` itself is deliberately NOT in this list: a worker's commit never creates
// an entry there -- the instance principal's `git worktree add` does -- so the parent needs traverse, not
// write, and a group-writable parent would let a worker create entries of its own. The entry UNDER it
// is what the commit writes (its own index, HEAD, reflog and ORIG_HEAD), and it is granted as a
// subtree where the attempt key lives.
// THE THREE SUBTREES, EACH AS ITS OWN ROW, so a subtree name is spelled once and the list below is built
// from the rows rather than being the place the names live. The walk target, the prune group, the sticky
// decision and the file-write decision are all keyed on these names; spelling them again at each use would
// let one of the four keys drift from the others -- which is precisely the bug class this follow-up keeps
// catching (a subtree that is walked but not judged, or judged and not walked).
data dashboard_git_worker_objects_child: NonEmptyStr = ".git/objects"
data dashboard_git_worker_refs_child: NonEmptyStr = ".git/refs"
data dashboard_git_worker_logs_child: NonEmptyStr = ".git/logs"

data dashboard_git_worker_directory_children: List<NonEmptyStr> = [
  dashboard_git_worker_objects_child, dashboard_git_worker_refs_child, dashboard_git_worker_logs_child,
] as List<NonEmptyStr>

// THE MEMBER LABEL THE THREE GIT-WORKER OWNED-DIRECTORY DEMANDS ARE MINTED WITH. This ONE function is
// the typed origin of "is this demand one of the git subtree's?": the roster mints members through it
// (dashboard_instance_worker_git_directories) and the apply partitions through it
// (is_dashboard_git_worker_directory_demand below, called from gunbc.live_deploy.apply_legs
// git_worker_subtree_demand_claimed), by PATH-FREE member equality -- never by a substring of the path,
// which would misclassify any unrelated owned directory whose path merely happens to contain /logs or
// /refs (review round: path-substring classifier).
fn dashboard_git_worker_directory_member(child: NonEmptyStr) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join(["git-worker-", child as String], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

// PARTITION AT THE MINT AUTHORITY (WORKER-ROOT-EQUIVALENT-3, typed-legs port). "Does this owned-directory
// demand belong to the git worker subtree?" is decided HERE, where the demands are minted, by PATH-FREE
// MEMBER EQUALITY against the single member authority above -- never by a substring of the path, which
// would misclassify any unrelated owned directory whose path merely contains /logs, /refs or /objects
// (review round: path-substring classifier). This is the predicate the standing directories plan EXCLUDES
// by and the subtree plan INCLUDES by, so the two partitions are complementary by construction: no
// instance of a demand is in both, and none is silently in neither (the standing plan installs everything
// this refuses). Lives beside the minter rather than in the apply because the apply may only ASK the
// source (DESIGN section 4: the richer source exists); the typed legs in gunbc.live_deploy.apply_legs call
// this, they do not re-derive it.
fn is_dashboard_git_worker_directory_demand(t: OwnedDirectoryTarget) -> Bool {
  any(
    xs: dashboard_git_worker_directory_children,
    predicate: child => owned_directory_member(t: t) == dashboard_git_worker_directory_member(child: child),
  )
}

// THE CHILD OF THE REPO ROOT UNDER WHICH EVERY ATTEMPT'S PRIVATE GIT ENTRY SITS. Owned by the
// account that creates the entries, world-traversable, never group-writable: git creates an entry there, and the worker's own
// commit only ever writes inside the one entry the unit's ReadWritePaths= names.
data dashboard_git_worktrees_child: NonEmptyStr = ".git/worktrees"

fn dashboard_instance_worker_git_directories(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> List<OwnedDirectoryTarget> {
  match instance.worker {
    WorkerIsServiceUser => []
    WorkerDedicatedAccount => map(dashboard_git_worker_directory_children, child => OwnedDirectoryDeclared {
      member: dashboard_git_worker_directory_member(child: child),
      path: dashboard_instance_child(root: instance.repo_root, child: child as String),
      owner: dashboard_instance_worker_writable_owner(instance: instance),
      // THE ONE MODE AUTHORITY (dashboard_git_worker_subtree_declared_directory_mode): objects/ installs
      // 3770 (the sticky arm, fencing the root-owned pack/ and info/ a group-writable parent would
      // otherwise expose to rename-over), refs/ and logs/ install 2770. This is the value `install -d -m`
      // carries and the value the readback's top leg is held to -- the same DeclaredDirectoryMode, so
      // there is no longer a second FileMode the two could disagree with (round 3 finding (4)).
      mode: dashboard_git_worker_subtree_declared_directory_mode(child: child),
      group_adoption: dashboard_instance_worker_writable_group_adoption(instance: instance),
    })
  }
}

// THE COMMIT WRITE-SET, AS DATA (WORKER-ROOT-EQUIVALENT-3). A `git commit` in a LINKED WORKTREE of a
// non-bare repository writes exactly these shapes under .git, and nothing else. Derived by running
// the commit and diffing the tree (git 2.43 and 2.47, detached from any host this repo models), not
// by reading git's documentation at one remove:
//
//   objects/<xx>/<rest>        loose objects. git creates a MISSING fan-out directory itself, so the
//                              PARENT (.git/objects) must be group-writable AND carry setgid, or the
//                              new fan-out lands in the wrong group; git creates the object file 0444
//                              under core.sharedRepository=group and 0444 is already read-only to all.
//   refs/heads/<branch>        the branch ref, written loose: 0664 under the shared posture.
//   logs/refs/heads/<branch>   the branch reflog, appended.
//   <entry>/…                  the worktree's OWN HEAD, index(+.lock), logs/HEAD, ORIG_HEAD,
//                              COMMIT_EDITMSG, under .git/worktrees/<key> -- see
//                              dispatch_attempt_worker_git_entry, and .git/COMMIT_EDITMSG for the
//                              `git commit` editor scratch, which git removes again.
//
// NOT WRITTEN, and each is a claim rather than an omission:
//   .git itself        never. `git commit` creates and unlinks .git/COMMIT_EDITMSG, and a linked
//                      worktree's HEAD/index live in the ENTRY, so the directory needs no write at
//                      all -- measured with it 0555, 25 consecutive commits.
//   packed-refs        never, for this write-set. A new branch is written LOOSE and a loose ref
//                      shadows the packed one; git rewrites packed-refs only for `pack-refs`, a
//                      branch delete, or the auto-GC/maintenance sweep. Those belong to the maintenance
//                      unit's principal -- dashboard_git_maintenance_principal, the instance's directory
//                      owner -- and the worker's own commit must not; the confined path list below says so.
//   config, HEAD, description, index (repo-level), hooks/, info/, branches/, objects/pack/,
//   objects/info/, .git/COMMIT_EDITMSG's parent grant -- all stay as #13942 left them.
data dashboard_git_worker_commit_write_set: List<NonEmptyStr> = [
  ".git/objects/<fan-out>/<object>", ".git/refs/heads/<branch>", ".git/logs/refs/heads/<branch>",
] as List<NonEmptyStr>

// THE CONFIG THE POSTURE NEEDS, written by the INSTANCE PRINCIPAL's step before any attempt worktree is added
// (extdeps/git git_shared_repository_group_config_argv). Two keys, both load-bearing and both
// measured:
//   core.sharedRepository=group  git's own shared-repository behaviour. Without it git creates under
//                                the process umask -- the fan-out directory 0755, the reflog 0644 --
//                                and a second writer arriving through the group cannot write at all.
//   gc.auto=0, maintenance.auto=false  `git commit` spawns `git maintenance run --auto`, which
//                                reaches for packed-refs.lock IN .git ITSELF -- a path #13942
//                                confines from the worker. The attempt still commits (the child's failure is
//                                a stderr error, not the commit's), but it is not noise the model
//                                should be teaching a worker to tolerate: the automatic repack is a
//                                maintenance job, so the auto trigger is switched off for the
//                                repository instead of the confined write being granted.
data dashboard_git_worker_local_config_keys: List<NonEmptyStr> = [
  "core.sharedRepository", "gc.auto", "maintenance.auto",
] as List<NonEmptyStr>

// WHAT MUST STAY OUT OF REACH of the group, named rather than left to the walk. This is #13942's
// carve-out restated as the thing the recursive step must never touch: .git itself and the entries
// that make up the repository's identity and behaviour. The claim
// (test.claim.roadmap.worker_dedicated_account_witness the_worker_commit_never_writes_inside_the_git_directory_itself)
// reads the derived write-set against this list, so a grant that ever reaches one of them fails here
// rather than on srv1.
data dashboard_git_worker_unwritable_children: List<NonEmptyStr> = [
  ".git", ".git/config", ".git/HEAD", ".git/description", ".git/index", ".git/COMMIT_EDITMSG",
  ".git/packed-refs", ".git/hooks", ".git/info", ".git/branches", ".git/objects/pack", ".git/objects/info",
] as List<NonEmptyStr>

fn dashboard_instance_worker_git_unwritable_paths(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> List<FilePath> {
  match instance.worker {
    WorkerIsServiceUser => []
    WorkerDedicatedAccount => map(
      dashboard_git_worker_unwritable_children,
      child => dashboard_instance_child(root: instance.repo_root, child: child as String),
    )
  }
}

// THE ONE-TIME MIGRATION OF THE EXISTING SUBTREE (WORKER-ROOT-EQUIVALENT-3, the fork the brief
// asked to be decided). REASON IT IS A MIGRATION AND NOT A RECURSIVE PROVIDER: the provider asserts
// one directory and does not recurse (gunbc.owned_directory ensure_owned_directory_provider_argv),
// and making it recurse is the wrong instrument here -- a recursive assert over .git would descend
// into hooks, info and objects/pack, which #13942 confines from the worker, and it would run on every apply
// over an object store that grows without bound. A migration runs ONCE, its readback is the
// receipt, and it can name what it must not touch. A hand step on srv1 is therefore not needed: the
// step below is the bounded, declared form of it, and its readback is the claim below, not a person.
//
// WHAT IT DOES, and the four constraints that make it bounded:
//   ONE, it re-GROUPS and never re-OWNS: chgrp with no chown argument, so the owner stays the service
//   user everywhere. Nothing the service user owns becomes worker-writable except through the group.
//   TWO, it walks ONLY the granted subtrees, and prunes the confined ones by NAME before descending,
//   so hooks, info, branches and the two objects/ subdirectories can never be reached even if the
//   grant list changes. (The walk also never follows a symlink: -P is find's default and the
//   predicate list contains no -L; chgrp/chmod -R act on the link itself, not its target, measured
//   against a link to /etc/passwd whose mode was unchanged.)
//   THREE, one file per argument: -print0 into xargs -0 -I, so no path can be split or reinterpreted.
//   FOUR, setgid on DIRECTORIES ONLY (find -type d), which is what makes a fan-out directory git
//   creates later inherit the attempt group instead of the creator's primary group.
//
// The directory mode a WALKED interior ends at is 2775 (3775 under the sticky arm) and the file mode 0664
// -- exactly what core.sharedRepository=group makes git ask for, so the migrated contents and the contents
// git creates later are one shape and neither route can leave a file the other cannot write. The TOP of each
// subtree is NOT the sweep's to make: the owned-directory provider installs it at the declared arm
// (02770/03770, file_mode_octal of dashboard_git_worker_subtree_granted_directory_mode) and the additive
// sweep preserves those bits -- which is why the wet fixture installs its tops the same way.
// THE MIGRATION STEPS. The group is the SAME value the declared demands carry
// (dashboard_instance_worker_writable_owner), so the migration cannot drift from the model it is
// preparing the ground for. THE PRUNES COME BEFORE THE WALK and restate the confinement: a granted
// subtree is never under one of those names, so the walk cannot reach hooks, info, branches or pack
// whatever the grant list says, and a later reader cannot "simplify" a prune away without the
// confinement claim noticing.
// WHICH CHILDREN OF A GRANTED SUBTREE ARE CONFINED, named RELATIVE to that subtree itself. objects
// carries the two confined children #13942 keeps out of the worker's reach; refs and logs carry NONE, and that is
// load-bearing: a prune name in refs would be a pattern, and a pattern prunes real refs (a branch
// named pack or branches) out of the walk forever. The builder splices each name under the target, so
// what is pruned is exactly <target>/pack and <target>/info and nothing at any other depth.
fn dashboard_git_worker_subtree_confined_children(child: NonEmptyStr) -> List<String> {
  if (child as String) == (dashboard_git_worker_objects_child as String) {
    ["pack", "info"]
  } else {
    []
  }
}

// HOW DEEP THE MIGRATION AND ITS READBACK MAY WALK, in one bounded step. `-maxdepth` is find's option,
// but the NUMBER is this deployment's policy, which is why it is declared here and passed down rather
// than fixed inside extdeps.tools.findutils: git's object fan-out is two hex characters (depth 2 below
// objects/), refs are arbitrarily nested user-named paths, and the walk is the migration's entire
// runtime -- so the bound is simultaneously a correctness bound (deep enough to reach every entry a
// commit can create) and a cost bound (a run-away walk over a repository is the migration hanging).
// 32 is comfortably past any real refs/ nesting and past the object fan-out, while keeping the walk
// bounded on a repository whose refs somebody has nested pathologically. THE WALK DEPTH IS NOT A
// GRANT: it bounds which existing entries are touched, and every path a commit needs is far shallower.
// An unbounded walk would be worse than a shallow one -- it would descend into a repository's own
// packed-refs-free history for no benefit, and a bounded one has a receipt that can be read in full.
data dashboard_git_worker_subtree_walk_depth: Nat = 32

fn dashboard_git_worker_subtree_chgrp_command(target: String, group: NonEmptyStr, pruned: List<String>) -> ArgvCommand {
  find_subtree_regroup_command(
    target: target,
    group: group as String,
    pruned: pruned,
    depth: dashboard_git_worker_subtree_walk_depth
  )
}

// WHICH GRANTED SUBTREES GET THE STICKY BIT, and the answer is deliberately not "all of them".
// +t is the bit that stops a group-writable PARENT from letting its writer rename or unlink entries
// it does not own, and it is needed exactly where such entries exist: .git/objects holds the two
// subtrees #13942 keeps out of the worker's reach (pack, info), and the maintenance job this PR names later
// WRITES AND DELETES THROUGH objects/pack (`git gc` runs `repack -d`). Without +t, one `mv
// objects/pack objects/pack.x && ln -s <worker's dir> objects/pack` from inside a confined attempt
// points that job at a directory the worker chose -- the owner writing and deleting where a worker
// directed it, which voids the confinement against the job it names (the host reading,
// 2026-10-18; rename(2) checks the parent, not the entry, so a 0555 objects/pack renames freely
// under a writable objects/). refs and logs get NO +t because they hold no entries the worker is
// forbidden to remove -- every ref and reflog there is created by an attempt, and attempts all run
// as the ONE account gunbc-worker, so there is no foreign-owned entry to protect and +t would buy
// nothing while adding a bit to read back. The per-attempt worktree entry is excluded for a
// different, harder reason: the instance principal's `git worktree add` checks the entry out, so
// its index and HEAD are entries owned by that principal, which the worker renames OVER on its
// first `git add` (index.lock -> index);
// +t there would make the very commit this PR enables fail with EPERM.
data dashboard_git_worker_sticky_children: List<NonEmptyStr> = [dashboard_git_worker_objects_child] as List<NonEmptyStr>

fn dashboard_git_worker_subtree_needs_sticky(child: NonEmptyStr) -> Bool {
  any(xs: dashboard_git_worker_sticky_children, predicate: c => (c as String) == (child as String))
}

// THE MODE AUTHORITY FOR THE THREE SHARED SUBTREES -- THE ONE PLACE A GRANTED TOP-LEVEL MODE IS DECIDED
// (WORKER-ROOT-EQUIVALENT-3, round 3 finding (4), bright-moth-475 2026-10-20: the declaration said 2770
// while the readback demanded 2775/3775 and the sweep adds but never clears bits, so
// assert-then-migrate left objects/ at 3770 and the readback refused its own route). There is now ONE
// named arm per subtree, in gunbc.managed_directory, and EVERY consumer renders from this function: the
// owned-directory declaration (what the deploy installs), the migration's sticky decision (the +t action
// below, still keyed on dashboard_git_worker_subtree_needs_sticky -- the SAME row this matches on), and
// the readback's top expectation (the fold's `top:` argument). A rename or a mode change moves all three
// together because they read the same two constructors; there is no second FileMode to forget.
// objects/ gets DirectoryOwnerGroupWritableSetgidSticky (3770): it holds root-owned entries (pack/, info/
// -- #13942's, which the named maintenance job writes and deletes through) in a directory the attempt
// group writes, and rename(2) checks the PARENT, so without +t a worker could mv root's objects/pack
// aside and plant a symlink the owner's repack then writes through (measured: EPERM under +t, succeeds
// without -- extdeps.tools.findutils find_subtree_group_writable_command carries the measurement).
// refs/ and logs/ get DirectoryOwnerGroupWritableSetgid (2770): every entry there belongs to an attempt
// and attempts are ONE account, so there is nothing foreign to fence and +t would only EPERM the worker's
// own lock-and-rename.
// THE OTHER LEG IS --- IN EVERY ARM: no principal outside {briansrls, gunbc-attempt} reads the lab repo --
// fleet_posix_accounts declares the worker's group membership as the CLOSED list [gunbc-attempt] and the
// instance's service user is briansrls, so those are the only readers (finding (b)'s answer, asked of the
// model rather than of preference). Interior entries keep whatever the host left them at: the sweep adds
// g+w/g+s/+t and NEVER touches o-bits, so a fan-out dir that arrived at 775 stays readable at 775-family;
// only the declared TOP is held to the arm exactly.
fn dashboard_git_worker_subtree_declared_directory_mode(child: NonEmptyStr) -> DeclaredDirectoryMode {
  if dashboard_git_worker_subtree_needs_sticky(child: child) {
    DirectoryOwnerGroupWritableSetgidSticky
  } else {
    DirectoryOwnerGroupWritableSetgid
  }
}

// WHICH SUBTREE HOLDS A FILE GIT WRITES IN PLACE, and therefore which one's files must be group-writable.
// DERIVED FROM THE WRITE-SET, NOT ASSUMED: git creates a loose object, a ref, an index and a HEAD by
// writing a temporary and RENAMING it over the target -- a rename is a write to the PARENT DIRECTORY, so
// the file's own mode is irrelevant to it (measured on git 2.47.3, 2026-10-19: four real linked-worktree
// commits, every object and ref file at git's own 0444, all rc=0). A REFLOG is the exception: git opens it
// in APPEND mode, and with an existing logs/refs/heads/<branch> at 0444 the commit dies with "unable to
// append ... Permission denied" (same measurement). So `.git/logs` -- the branch reflogs, which arrive
// with the repository at 0644 and are the ONLY pre-existing files a worker's commit writes IN PLACE -- is
// the one repository subtree whose files need g+w. objects/ and refs/ are NOT on this list, and that is a
// real narrowness rather than an omission: their files belong to the serve account, an object is
// content-addressed, and a group-writable base object is one any attempt could rewrite in place,
// corrupting it for every attempt that ever reads the same hash. The per-attempt worktree entry carries
// its own reflog (logs/HEAD) and is covered by dashboard_git_worker_entry_writes_files_in_place.
data dashboard_git_worker_appended_file_children: List<NonEmptyStr> = [dashboard_git_worker_logs_child] as List<NonEmptyStr>

fn dashboard_git_worker_subtree_writes_files_in_place(child: NonEmptyStr) -> Bool {
  any(xs: dashboard_git_worker_appended_file_children, predicate: c => (c as String) == (child as String))
}

// THE PER-ATTEMPT WORKTREE ENTRY WRITES FILES IN PLACE, so its files DO get g+w: the entry holds a
// reflog of its own (logs/HEAD), which the worker's commit appends to, and its index and HEAD are
// replaced by rename -- which needs the parent's write, granted by the directory mode, not the file's.
// It is a separate row from dashboard_git_worker_subtree_writes_files_in_place because the entry is not
// one of dashboard_git_worker_directory_children: no apply of an instance knows its attempts in advance.
fn dashboard_git_worker_entry_writes_files_in_place() -> Bool {
  true
}

// Group-writable on the DIRECTORIES the walk reaches, setgid on them (so a fan-out directory git creates
// later inherits the attempt group), the sticky bit where the subtree holds entries the walker must not
// remove (dashboard_git_worker_subtree_needs_sticky) -- and the FILES group-writable only where this
// subtree holds a file git appends to (dashboard_git_worker_subtree_writes_files_in_place), which is a
// per-subtree decision the caller passes rather than a constant the tool decides. See that row for why.
fn dashboard_git_worker_subtree_chmod_command(
  target: String,
  pruned: List<String>,
  sticky: Bool,
  files_group_writable: Bool,
) -> ArgvCommand {
  find_subtree_group_writable_command(
    target: target,
    pruned: pruned,
    sticky: sticky,
    files_group_writable: files_group_writable,
    depth: dashboard_git_worker_subtree_walk_depth
  )
}

fn dashboard_git_worker_subtree_path(instance: HostDashboardInstance, child: NonEmptyStr) -> String {
  dashboard_instance_child(root: instance.repo_root, child: child as String) as String
}

// THE ORDERED MIGRATION for one instance: re-group every shared subtree, then assert its modes.
// Empty on the staged arm, so prod gets no step at all (WorkerIsServiceUser). The per-attempt entry
// is NOT here -- it is the dispatch actuator's, next to the attempt key, at the spawn that creates
// it.
//
// THE GROUP IS A PARAMETER BECAUSE IT IS A HOST FACT THE CALLER OWNS, not a constant the migration
// may invent: every PRODUCTION call site binds fleet_posix_worker_shared_group_name (the row
// fleet_posix_accounts declares, and the account creation this slice is built beside), and the wet
// witness binds the group the runner can actually resolve -- a test on a machine it does not
// administer cannot chgrp to a group the host has never been told about, and `find` fails closed when
// it cannot (exit 1, verified). NO DEFAULT ARGUMENT: a default would let a call site silently take the
// production name where it meant to name the host's group, which is a fabricated value (DESIGN 5). The
// dry pairing claim the_production_route_names_the_declared_group_in_every_grant_command keeps the
// production binding executed and asserted, so the substituted token is never the only route through
// this code.
// ONE SUBTREE'S re-group and mode grant, as the two argv that give it. This is the single place the
// grant is spelled: the whole-repo migration maps the confined children through it, AND the live_deploy
// apply emits one member per child through it (so the apply and the plan-side migration cannot drift).
// It carries NO WorkerDedicatedAccount gate -- the caller has already made the dedicated-worker
// decision; a per-child command is meaningful for whatever child and group it is handed.
fn dashboard_git_worker_subtree_child_migration(
  instance: HostDashboardInstance,
  child: NonEmptyStr,
  group: NonEmptyStr,
) -> List<ArgvCommand> {
  [
    dashboard_git_worker_subtree_chgrp_command(
      target: dashboard_git_worker_subtree_path(instance: instance, child: child),
      group: group,
      pruned: dashboard_git_worker_subtree_confined_children(child: child),
    ),
    dashboard_git_worker_subtree_chmod_command(
      target: dashboard_git_worker_subtree_path(instance: instance, child: child),
      pruned: dashboard_git_worker_subtree_confined_children(child: child),
      sticky: dashboard_git_worker_subtree_needs_sticky(child: child),
      files_group_writable: dashboard_git_worker_subtree_writes_files_in_place(child: child),
    ),
  ]
}

fn dashboard_instance_worker_git_subtree_migration(
  instance: HostDashboardInstance,
  group: NonEmptyStr,
) -> List<ArgvCommand> {
  match instance.worker {
    WorkerIsServiceUser => []
    // THE DECLARED WHOLE-REPO ORDER: re-group EVERY subtree first, then grant the modes on every
    // subtree. That order is itself part of the contract the claims assert (group membership must be
    // in place before anything is made group-writable, so a step never widens a wrong-group tree). A
    // per-member apply takes the per-child pair instead -- within one member the chgrp still precedes
    // its chmod, which is the same rule at member scale.
    WorkerDedicatedAccount => concat(
      map(
        dashboard_git_worker_directory_children,
        child => dashboard_git_worker_subtree_chgrp_command(
          target: dashboard_git_worker_subtree_path(instance: instance, child: child),
          group: group,
          pruned: dashboard_git_worker_subtree_confined_children(child: child),
        ),
      ),
      map(
        dashboard_git_worker_directory_children,
        child => dashboard_git_worker_subtree_chmod_command(
          target: dashboard_git_worker_subtree_path(instance: instance, child: child),
          pruned: dashboard_git_worker_subtree_confined_children(child: child),
          sticky: dashboard_git_worker_subtree_needs_sticky(child: child),
          files_group_writable: dashboard_git_worker_subtree_writes_files_in_place(child: child),
        ),
      ),
    )
  }
}

// THE ROOT-OWNED REPOSITORY MAINTENANCE JOB (WORKER-ROOT-EQUIVALENT-3). Switching the automatic
// repack off is a CONSUMER OBLIGATION under DESIGN section 3c: a deferred trigger needs an owner, or
// loose objects and packs in the lab repository grow without bound -- a cost-shape defect, not a
// style point. THE JOB IS NAMED HERE, beside the migration and the two keys that defer to it, rather
// than left as prose:
//
//   WHAT        `git -C <repo> gc --quiet`, then `git -C <repo> pack-refs --all`. Both rewrite
//               packed-refs and both write under .git/objects/pack -- the exact paths #13942 keeps
//               out of the worker's reach, which is why no attempt can run them and why deferring to
//               the instance's own maintenance principal is the only shape that closes the noise
//               rather than granting a confined write.
//   AS WHOM     THE INSTANCE'S OWN PRINCIPAL -- instance.directory_owner.user, the same account the
//               serve runs as, the same account the enrolled SSH user is, and the owner of every entry
//               in the repository. NOT root and NOT the worker. Measured on srv1 (bright-moth-475,
//               2026-10-17): the lab serve is a SYSTEM unit running as the directory owner, the
//               actuator's `git worktree add` runs as that same account (it is a spawn command of the
//               serve, not a privileged step), and EVERY entry under objects/refs/logs and all 185
//               .git/worktrees/<key> entries are owned by it. So ownership already sits there, and a
//               repack that runs as anyone else is a stranger to the tree it is packing.
//               An earlier cut of this row said "root", and so did the surrounding rationale; that was
//               read off a BuildBuddy runner whose uid happens to be 0, not off the lab (DESIGN 4d:
//               a figure or a principal must name where it was measured). It matters beyond prose:
//               the sticky bit on objects/ is what makes the owner's `gc` safe, and the owner is the
//               ONLY principal that may unlink or rmdir the worker-created entries it removes.
//   ON WHAT     a systemd timer, 03:30 daily with a randomized delay, matching the shape of the
//               machine's other opportunistic sweep (gunbc.fleet.fleet_converge_timer). Nightly and
//               untimed-at-boot so the sweep never competes with an attempt's worktree add.
//   ITS POSTURE unprivileged beyond file ownership: NoNewPrivileges, PrivateTmp, ProtectSystem=strict
//               with the instance's repo root the only write target. It is not a dispatch unit and
//               shares nothing with the worker's contract.
//
// THE FRONTIER, declared rather than implied: THIS PR DECLARES the unit; it does not install it. The
// remaining step is the apply that writes the two unit files and enables the timer, owned by
// gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_dashboard_instance_apply beside the migration argv -- which is exactly where
// the migration's own argv is consumed, so the two land together or not at all. Until then the lab
// repository accumulates loose objects at the rate attempts commit, which is the cost the timer
// bounds. The claim (test.claim.roadmap.worker_dedicated_account_witness
// the_worker_never_rewrites_packed_refs_and_the_repack_has_one_named_owner) pins the pair: the number of deferred keys equals the
// number of keys the maintenance job's own argv re-enables, and a unit whose principal is not the
// instance's own directory owner -- nor the confined worker account -- is refused rather than assumed.
data dashboard_git_maintenance_unit_stem: NonEmptyStr = "git-maintenance"
data dashboard_git_maintenance_service_suffix: String = ".service"
data dashboard_git_maintenance_timer_suffix: String = ".timer"
data dashboard_git_maintenance_timer_on_calendar: String = "*-*-* 03:30:00"
// THE PRINCIPAL IS READ FROM THE INSTANCE, not asserted. A constant "root" here would have been a
// second, unfalsifiable story about the host: it claimed a privilege the lab apply does not have (its
// exec route is an SSH command as the enrolled user) and it denied the one the lab actually has (the
// entries are the directory owner's). Deriving it also makes the unit land correctly on a host whose
// owner is not the fleet default, and it is what the claim pins against instance.directory_owner.user.
fn dashboard_git_maintenance_principal(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> NonEmptyStr {
  instance.directory_owner.user
}

fn dashboard_git_maintenance_service_unit_name(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> String {
  join(
    [
      "gunbc-dashboard-", instance.instance_id as String, "-",
      dashboard_git_maintenance_unit_stem as String, dashboard_git_maintenance_service_suffix,
    ],
    "",
  )
}

fn dashboard_git_maintenance_timer_unit_name(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> String {
  join(
    [
      "gunbc-dashboard-", instance.instance_id as String, "-",
      dashboard_git_maintenance_unit_stem as String, dashboard_git_maintenance_timer_suffix,
    ],
    "",
  )
}

// The unit's ExecStart pair, as the argv it will be rendered from. Both are nulled the way every
// other managed git invocation is nulled: an ambient operator config must not be able to redirect
// the sweep to another repository, and an ambient `gc.auto` must not make the nightly sweep
// conditional on a threshold nobody declared.
fn dashboard_git_maintenance_exec_commands(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> List<ArgvCommand> {
  match instance.worker {
    WorkerIsServiceUser => []
    WorkerDedicatedAccount => [
      git_repack_quiet_command(repo: instance.repo_root),
      git_pack_refs_all_command(repo: instance.repo_root),
    ]
  }
}

// The keys the maintenance job OWNS, i.e. the deferred auto triggers it stands in for. Exactly the
// last two of dashboard_git_worker_local_config_keys: core.sharedRepository is not a repack trigger
// and no maintenance task re-enables it.
data dashboard_git_maintenance_owned_config_keys: List<NonEmptyStr> = [
  "gc.auto", "maintenance.auto",
] as List<NonEmptyStr>

// THE MAINTENANCE UNIT DOCUMENTS, AT THEIR SINGLE HOME (WORKER-ROOT-EQUIVALENT-3, moved onto the
// live_deploy route by bright-moth-475's ruling msg_32acd088). They live HERE, beside the exec argv
// they run and the two keys that defer to them, because they are consumed by TWO routes that must not
// disagree about their bytes: the deploy emitter (gunbc.live_deploy.emit) writes and installs them, and
// the deploy OBSERVER (gunbc.live_deploy.member_observe) hashes the very same rendering to decide
// whether the host already holds the repository posture. If the document were spelled in emit, the
// observer could only re-derive it by copying -- a second authority for one fact. Both modules import
// THIS one, so the serialization happens once.
//
// THE PAIR IS ONE FACT WITH THE TWO DEFERRAL KEYS, not a separate obligation: gc.auto=0 and
// maintenance.auto=false switch off the only repack the repository otherwise runs, and this unit is the
// owner that repack needs back (DESIGN 3c: a deferred trigger names an owner, or the cost degrades
// externally). They are hashed together into one member identity (RepositorySharedPosture) so a deploy
// can never turn the automatic repack off without installing the scheduled one -- the two halves cannot
// drift across deploys because there is nothing to drift: one digest covers all five things.
//
// AS WHOM: dashboard_git_maintenance_principal -- the instance's OWN directory owner, the account the
// serve runs as and that already owns every entry in the tree. NOT root, NOT the worker (see the
// measured frontier above; DESIGN 4d). It is a system timer, installed and enabled like the belt's
// credential-recovery timer (the precedent for an instance-scoped timer on this route).
fn dashboard_git_maintenance_service_unit_file(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> SystemdUnitFile {
  ServiceUnitFile {
    unit: [
      Description { text: "gunbc dashboard instance git repository maintenance (owner repack)" as NonEmptyStr },
    ],
    service: concat([
      ServiceType { mode: OneshotMode },
      ServiceUser { name: dashboard_git_maintenance_principal(instance: instance) },
      ServiceGroup { name: dashboard_git_maintenance_principal(instance: instance) },
      WorkingDirectory { path: instance.repo_root },
    ], map(dashboard_git_maintenance_exec_commands(instance: instance),
      c => ExecStart { command: join(argv_words(command: c), " ") as NonEmptyStr })),
    install: NotInstallable,
  }
}

fn dashboard_git_maintenance_timer_unit_file(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> SystemdUnitFile {
  TimerUnitFile {
    unit: [
      Description { text: "Daily gunbc dashboard instance git repository maintenance" as NonEmptyStr },
    ],
    timer: [
      OnCalendar { expression: dashboard_git_maintenance_timer_on_calendar as NonEmptyStr },
      Persistent { enabled: false },
    ],
    install: Installable { directives: [WantedBy { target: "timers.target" as NonEmptyStr }] },
  }
}

// THE UNIT FILES' HOME ON THE HOST, spelled like every other unit of this deployment (the /etc/systemd/
// system directory deployment_systemd_unit_path uses). Named here so the observer reads at exactly the
// path the emitter installs to; one authority for the destination.
fn dashboard_git_maintenance_service_unit_path(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join(["/etc/systemd/system/", dashboard_git_maintenance_service_unit_name(instance: instance)], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

fn dashboard_git_maintenance_timer_unit_path(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join(["/etc/systemd/system/", dashboard_git_maintenance_timer_unit_name(instance: instance)], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

// A (key, value) PAIR as the posture document is built from it -- NOT GitLocalConfigExpectation, which
// names its second field `expected` and belongs to a WRITE's declaration. The document is hashed on both
// sides from the SAME rendering: the desired side feeds the declared expectations, the observer side
// feeds the values git reported. A pair is the neutral carrier both sides can hold.
type DashboardGitConfigPosturePair {
  key: NonEmptyStr
  value: String
}

// THE REPOSITORY POSTURE AS ONE DOCUMENT (WORKER-ROOT-EQUIVALENT-3). The three local config keys and
// the two maintenance unit files, rendered into one canonical text and hashed to a single digest. The
// ORDER is part of the identity: keys in the order extdeps.git's git_shared_repository_posture_readbacks
// declares them (the same rows that drive the WRITES -- renaming a key moves the document with it), then
// the service unit, then the timer. Each entry is delimited (a `key=value` line, a `service-unit=` line,
// a `timer-unit=` line) so a value cannot bleed into the next entry's bytes.
//
// THE CALLER FEEDS THE CONTENT; THIS ONLY LAYS IT OUT. The desired side passes the declared pairs and
// the declared serializations; the OBSERVER side passes the values git actually reported and the unit
// files it actually read off disk. Hashing the SAME rendering of each side and comparing is what makes
// the digest an honest standing: if a unit file on the host drifts from what we mean to install, or a key
// is missing, or an extra key was set, the digest moves and the member re-asserts. The layout is here so
// the two sides cannot disagree about SEPARATORS while agreeing about content -- one authority for the
// bytes, two honest feeders.
//
// WHY ONE DIGEST OVER FIVE THINGS: these five are one obligation (the deferred repack and its owner),
// and DESIGN 3 says one concept one authority. Hashing them together is what stops a deploy from
// half-applying the obligation -- if the units change but the keys do not, or vice versa, the digest
// moves and the member re-asserts, because the document is the member's whole standing.
fn dashboard_git_shared_repository_posture_document(
  pairs: List<DashboardGitConfigPosturePair>,
  service_unit_document: String,
  timer_unit_document: String,
) -> String {
  let key_section = map(pairs, p => join([p.key as String, "=", p.value], ""))
  let unit_section = [
    join(["service-unit=", service_unit_document], ""),
    join(["timer-unit=", timer_unit_document], ""),
  ]
  join(concat(key_section, unit_section), "\n")
}

// THE DECLARED POSTURE PAIRS: the (key, value) pairs the posture is SUPPOSED to hold, straight off the
// rows that drive the writes. Renaming a key or changing a value moves this and the write together.
fn dashboard_git_shared_repository_posture_declared_pairs() -> List<DashboardGitConfigPosturePair> {
  map(git_shared_repository_posture_readbacks, r =>
    DashboardGitConfigPosturePair { key: r.key, value: r.expected })
}

// THE DESIRED POSTURE DOCUMENT: declared pairs + declared unit serializations, laid out once.
fn dashboard_git_shared_repository_posture_desired_document(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> String {
  dashboard_git_shared_repository_posture_document(
    pairs: dashboard_git_shared_repository_posture_declared_pairs(),
    service_unit_document: serialize_systemd_unit_file(file: dashboard_git_maintenance_service_unit_file(instance: instance)),
    timer_unit_document: serialize_systemd_unit_file(file: dashboard_git_maintenance_timer_unit_file(instance: instance)),
  )
}

// THE GIT-CONFIG KEY READBACKS the posture member asks git for, in the order that builds the document.
// Derived from the SAME readback rows, so the member's observation legs are tied to the writes.
fn dashboard_git_worker_posture_readback_keys() -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  map(git_shared_repository_posture_readbacks, r => r.key)
}


// THE MODES A COMMIT REQUIRES, stated as FileMode -- the type extdeps.access.posix already owns for
// this concept (Open Group sys_stat.h), with file_mode_octal to render it and file_mode_of_octal_text
// to read a `find -printf %m` / `stat %a` observation back. An earlier cut of this slice declared the
// bits as Int constants and peeled them with `mode / step % 8`, which re-invented that authority for
// no benefit (DESIGN 2: a fresh authority for a concept that exists is a failed decomposition) and
// added a second grammar for the same four digits -- one parse in extdeps.access.posix, one here,
// guaranteed to disagree the first time a special bit mattered. So: the rwx triples and the setgid /
// sticky bits are NAMED, the predicates read fields, and the octal spelling exists exactly once, as
// what file_mode_octal renders. There is no decimal literal in this slice and no comment restating an
// octal in decimal, because there is nothing to restate -- the octal text is the rendering, not a
// comment on a number.
//   02770  the granted DIRECTORY top (managed_directory.DirectoryOwnerGroupWritableSetgid): owner and
//          attempt group rwx, other ---, SETGID -- the bit that makes a fan-out directory git creates
//          later inherit the attempt group rather than the creating process's primary group, which is
//          the whole reason a worker can write into objects/<xx>/.
//   03770  the granted directory top that HOLDS ROOT-OWNED ENTRIES (.git/objects: pack/ and info/ are
//          #13942's, and the named maintenance job writes and deletes through them). Sticky is the
//          companion to g+w, not an extra: in a group-writable directory without it, rename(2) checks
//          only the PARENT, so a confined worker can move root's objects/pack aside and plant a symlink
//          for the maintenance job to write through. See dashboard_git_worker_sticky_children for why
//          exactly one subtree gets it. THE OTHER LEG IS --- AND STAYS --- AT THE TOP: no principal
//          outside {instance service user, gunbc-attempt} reads the lab repo (fleet_posix_accounts
//          declares the worker's groups as the closed list [gunbc-attempt]). INTERIORS are not held to
//          an octal: the sweep only ADDS g+w/g+s/+t and never touches o-bits, so an interior that
//          arrived from the host at 0755/0775 keeps its readable other-leg -- see
//          dashboard_git_worker_interior_directory_mode_is_accepted for what IS demanded there.
//   0664   the granted FILE: owner and group rw, other r-- . A loose object is 0444 and stays that
//          way -- git writes it read-only on purpose and nobody needs to write it, which is why the
//          file predicate does not accept 0444 and does not need to.
//   0775   THE MEASURED PRE-MIGRATION STATE, and the reason this slice exists: every leg is right and
//          there is no setgid, so the next fan-out directory git creates inherits the creating
//          process's primary group and the worker cannot write into it. Read off srv1, 2026-10-17.
fn dashboard_git_permission_bits(r: Bool, w: Bool, x: Bool) -> PermissionBits {
  PermissionBits { read: r, write: w, execute: x }
}

data dashboard_git_worker_owner_group_full_bits: PermissionBits = dashboard_git_permission_bits(r: true, w: true, x: true)
data dashboard_git_worker_other_read_execute_bits: PermissionBits = dashboard_git_permission_bits(r: true, w: false, x: true)
data dashboard_git_worker_owner_group_write_bits: PermissionBits = dashboard_git_permission_bits(r: true, w: true, x: false)
data dashboard_git_worker_other_read_bits: PermissionBits = dashboard_git_permission_bits(r: true, w: false, x: false)

// THE TWO GRANTED TOPS ARE RENDERINGS OF THE DECLARED ARMS, not hand-built FileModes (round 3, finding
// (4)): 02770 and 03770, rendered by managed_directory.declared_directory_mode over the SAME two arms
// dashboard_git_worker_subtree_declared_directory_mode picks. An earlier cut spelled these as literals at
// 02775/03775 while the owned-directory declaration demanded 02770 from managed_directory -- two
// authorities for one mode, and the route that installs-then-sweeps landed at 2770/3770 and REFUSED ITSELF
// at the readback (the lab deploy of 0ed9fbca802, where the migration never ran and the tops sat at the
// asserted 2770). One row per mode now, in managed_directory; this module only renders and reads fields.
data dashboard_git_worker_granted_directory_mode: FileMode = declared_directory_mode(m: DirectoryOwnerGroupWritableSetgid)

data dashboard_git_worker_granted_shared_directory_mode: FileMode = declared_directory_mode(m: DirectoryOwnerGroupWritableSetgidSticky)



// THE MEASURED STATE, as a row rather than a decimal: it is EVIDENCE read off srv1, and the number
// that appears in the PR body and in the readback claim has to be the same one this model refuses.
data dashboard_git_worker_unmigrated_directory_mode: FileMode = FileMode {
  owner: dashboard_git_worker_owner_group_full_bits,
  group: dashboard_git_worker_owner_group_full_bits,
  other: dashboard_git_worker_other_read_execute_bits,
  setuid: false,
  setgid: false,
  sticky: false,
}

// TWO FILE MODES ARE ONE MODE WHEN THEY RENDER THE SAME. Structural comparison over the nine rwx
// fields plus the three special bits, expressed through the one renderer extdeps.access.posix owns:
// writing it as `a == b` would compare records field by field and silently accept a FileMode built
// with a setuid bit nobody observed, and writing it as decimal equality is what this replaces.
fn dashboard_git_worker_file_modes_are_equal(a: FileMode, b: FileMode) -> Bool {
  file_mode_octal(mode: a) == file_mode_octal(mode: b)
}

// THE GRANTED DIRECTORY MODE FOR ONE SUBTREE, rendered from the arm
// dashboard_git_worker_subtree_declared_directory_mode picks: 03770 where entries owned by someone other
// than the worker live beneath it, 02770 where none does. PER SUBTREE, not one global answer -- a single
// "is it 02770" predicate would make the sticky fix invisible to the model, because the readback would
// refuse the tree that is actually correct. This is the ONE rendering shared by the provider's `install -d
// -m`, the fold's exact top leg, and (through the octal text below) any witness that has to name a top's
// mode in a receipt line.
fn dashboard_git_worker_subtree_granted_directory_mode(child: NonEmptyStr) -> FileMode {
  if dashboard_git_worker_subtree_needs_sticky(child: child) {
    dashboard_git_worker_granted_shared_directory_mode
  } else {
    dashboard_git_worker_granted_directory_mode
  }
}

// A GRANTED TOP ARM, AS THE TEXT `find` PRINTS. The readback fold parses a receipt's mode column with
// file_mode_of_octal_text and compares it against an arm; anything OUTSIDE the fold that has to NAME a
// top's mode in text -- the wet witness's receipt legs, which observe one line each while the fold owns
// the decision -- has to render THAT arm, never re-spell a figure. The round-3 defect this correction
// exists for is exactly a witness whose hand-typed "3775"/"2775" outlived the arm it was transcribed
// from, so that the tree the route produced and the line the witness expected no longer named the same
// mode. Four digits always -- file_mode_octal renders the special digit too -- which is the shape
// find(1)'s %m prints when a special bit is set.
fn dashboard_git_worker_directory_mode_octal(mode: FileMode) -> String {
  file_mode_octal(mode: mode)
}

// THE TOP LEG'S EXPECTATION FOR ONE REPOSITORY SUBTREE, in the text above.
fn dashboard_git_worker_subtree_granted_directory_mode_octal(child: NonEmptyStr) -> String {
  dashboard_git_worker_directory_mode_octal(
    mode: dashboard_git_worker_subtree_granted_directory_mode(child: child),
  )
}

// THE PLAIN GRANT, and it REFUSES the sticky shape -- deliberately, and a claim leg pins it: the sticky
// shape is only correct for the one subtree that holds foreign-owned entries, so a predicate that
// accepted both would let refs/ or logs/ drift to the sticky mode unnoticed (where +t would EPERM the
// worker's own first `git add` against the entry's own index/HEAD). Ask the per-subtree question when the
// subtree is known; ask this one when the question is "is this the plain grant".
fn dashboard_git_worker_directory_mode_is_granted(mode: FileMode) -> Bool {
  dashboard_git_worker_file_modes_are_equal(a: mode, b: dashboard_git_worker_granted_directory_mode)
}

// IS A DIRECTORY *BENEATH* A GRANTED SUBTREE ACCEPTED? THE GRANTED BITS PLUS THE SAFETY FLOOR, NOT AN
// EXACT MODE -- and this is where the second authority used to live (round 3, finding (4)): an exact
// {02775, 03775} window is what a tree looks like when the walk reached a directory the HOST had left at
// 0755/0775, but the bits the host owns are not ours to certify. What OUR route guarantees on every
// directory it swept is exactly two things -- group-writable and setgid (plus +t in objects/) -- and what
// #13942's confinement requires of anything inside a granted subtree is the floor: never world-writable,
// never setuid. The other leg is whatever umask left: a fan-out directory git creates tomorrow inherits
// S_ISGID from its parent but NOT S_ISVTX, and git's adjust_shared_perm(core.sharedRepository=group) then
// raises only the group triple -- so the honest expected mode under a 03770 parent is the granted bits at
// whatever the creating process's umask gave `other` (measured on the host: mkdir under a sticky parent
// -> 2755, shared perm -> 2775; under the asserted 03770 the same walk yields the granted bits with the
// host's other-leg). Demanding an exact `other` triple here would (i) certify the host's umask as if our
// route had set it and (ii) refuse a correctly-granted tree whose fan-out dirs arrived at a different
// umask, which is a refusal of reality, not of a defect (DESIGN 5's inverse: the model must not fail on
// states it never promised). Sticky is DON'T-CARE: sweep-touched interiors under objects/ carry it,
// git-created fan-out dirs cannot inherit it, and both are granted.
fn dashboard_git_worker_interior_directory_mode_is_accepted(mode: FileMode) -> Bool {
  mode.group.write && mode.setgid && !mode.other.write && !mode.setuid
}

// A GRANTED FILE is group-writable, NOT world-writable, and carries NEITHER special bit -- three
// fields read as the consequences they state rather than as a peeled digit. `group.write` is the grant
// the worker needs; `other.write` is the thing #13942's confinement refuses, so 0666 is out and 0444 --
// a loose object as git first writes it, before the migration's sweep reaches it -- is out through the
// first conjunct.
//
// AND SETGID/STICKY ARE OUT, which is not a leftover from the classifier rewrite but the half of the
// rule the classifier made visible. The readback's file leg is the ONLY thing standing between a tree
// and a green receipt for an entry that is not a file at all: a line reading `f 2775 f9/obj` is a
// regular file wearing the directory grant, and a predicate that read only the rwx fields would report
// it granted. The modes git itself creates inside a granted subtree are 0664 (refs, reflogs, the
// worktree entry's files) and 0444-then-0664 (loose objects, after the sweep); none carries a special
// bit, because chmod only ever sets g+s on `-type d` lines (see find_subtree_group_writable_command --
// setgid on a file is a privilege shape, S_ISGID on an executable makes it set-group-id-to-the-group-of-
// its-owner). So a file WITH the bit is not a state the granted tree is ever in: accepting it would make
// the readback pass over exactly the tree the migration was written to refuse. (A claim leg pins the
// same refusal at the predicate itself, so it cannot quietly widen again.)
fn dashboard_git_worker_file_mode_is_granted(mode: FileMode) -> Bool {
  mode.group.write && !mode.other.write && !mode.setuid && !mode.setgid && !mode.sticky
}

// THE FILE LEG FOR A SUBTREE WHOSE FILES GIT NEVER OPENS FOR WRITING. DERIVED FROM THE SAME WRITE-SET
// MEASUREMENT as dashboard_git_worker_appended_file_children: objects, refs, an index and a HEAD are
// replaced by lock-and-rename, which asks the PARENT DIRECTORY for write and the file for nothing -- so
// demanding group-write there would (i) be wider than the commit needs and (ii) refuse the tree git is
// actually in: a loose object git writes under core.sharedRepository=group comes out at 0444 (measured,
// git 2.47.3 -- the group triple only reaches the mode umask left readable, and 0444 has none to add),
// while a ref cloned from a remote is 0644 and one written under the posture is 0664. What this leg DOES
// refuse is the two shapes that are never legitimate on a file, wherever they come from: world-write, and
// a special bit -- setgid on an executable file is how a lab turns a group grant into a privilege. It
// accepts 0444 and 0644 and refuses 02775, which is the pair the appended leg also refuses, so a setgid
// file cannot pass either branch of the readback.
fn dashboard_git_worker_readonly_file_mode_is_accepted(mode: FileMode) -> Bool {
  !mode.other.write && !mode.setgid && !mode.sticky
}

// THE PRE-MIGRATION STATE, stated as its negation so a claim can point at it directly: what srv1
// measured is NOT a grant. Its group leg is right (7) and it has no setgid -- which is exactly how a
// directory can look shared and still be unwritable by the attempt group for anything created after
// the migration.
fn dashboard_git_worker_directory_mode_is_unmigrated(mode: FileMode) -> Bool {
  dashboard_git_worker_file_modes_are_equal(a: mode, b: dashboard_git_worker_unmigrated_directory_mode)
}

// THE SHARED-REPOSITORY POSTURE, AS ONE ROW OF COMMANDS THE INSTANCE OWNS. It lives HERE, beside the
// op that carries it and the migration that follows it, because it is a fact about the INSTANCE's
// repository, not about a dispatch: the apply runs it and the dispatch actuator's spawn-side helper is
// a call to this. WHAT IT IS: core.sharedRepository=group must be in the config BEFORE any attempt
// worktree is added -- without it git creates the attempt's private git entry 0755 with 0644 files and
// no amount of later chgrp helps, because the entry's own files were opened without the group bit --
// and the two automatic repack triggers are switched off for the repository because the sweep they
// would start reaches packed-refs.lock in .git itself, which the worker's unit confines (see
// dashboard_git_maintenance_exec_commands for who does the sweeping instead).
//
// NONE OF IT IS A WORKER WRITE. Each command writes the repo's own .git/config, and the worker has no
// write there (#13942); they run under the instance principal -- the account the apply's enrolled SSH
// user is on the lab, which is also the repository's owner.
fn dashboard_ensure_git_shared_repository_posture(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> List<ArgvCommand> {
  match instance.worker {
    WorkerIsServiceUser => []
    WorkerDedicatedAccount => [
      git_shared_repository_group_config_command(repo: instance.repo_root),
      git_gc_auto_disabled_config_command(repo: instance.repo_root),
      git_maintenance_auto_disabled_config_command(repo: instance.repo_root),
    ]
  }
}

// WHAT ONE LINE OF THE WALK CAN BE: the subtree's OWN directory, a directory beneath it, or a file.
// THREE CASES, not two booleans. `is_directory` beside `is_top` would admit a
// fourth state (top, and not a directory) that the parser never built but nothing prevented anyone else
// from building, and it left the fold re-deriving the three-way choice by hand. DESIGN 4b's fourth rung
// is that the invalid state has NO CONSTRUCTOR; declaring the sum is what buys it, and the fold then
// matches instead of testing.
//
// THE MODE IS A FileMode, PARSED AS THE LINE IS READ, not text carried to the fold: a line whose mode the
// model cannot parse is not a line at all (the parser yields `none`), so the fold is never handed an
// unreadable mode and left to notice.
//
// THE KIND COMES FROM find's `%y` LETTER, never from the mode -- see find_subtree_mode_readback_command
// for why the execute bit cannot carry it (a setgid FILE sets it). A letter outside the two
// the model names is read as NOTHING, because a symlink, socket or device inside a git object store is
// not a granted entry, and guessing which of the two it was would be DESIGN 5's fabricated verdict. The
// subtree's own directory is the line whose `%P` came back EMPTY, and only a directory can be the start
// point -- so a file claiming the empty path is not a line either.
// EVERY CASE CARRIES THE GROUP, because every case was chgrp'd by the migration and a line without a
// group would be a line whose group half of the grant was never observed. NonEmptyStr, not String: find
// prints at least the numeric gid for every entry it can stat, so an empty group token is not a state of
// the world -- it is a line the parser did not read.
type DashboardGitSubtreeReadbackLine
  = ReadbackSubtreeTop {
      mode: FileMode,
      group: NonEmptyStr,
    }
  | ReadbackInteriorDirectory {
      mode: FileMode,
      group: NonEmptyStr,
      path: NonEmptyStr,
    }
  | ReadbackEntryFile {
      mode: FileMode,
      group: NonEmptyStr,
      path: NonEmptyStr,
    }

// THE ONE PARSER: `<letter> <mode> <group> <path>`, split on the separator with the PATH TAKEN AS
// EVERYTHING AFTER THE THREE PRINTED FIELDS so a path holding a space comes back whole. Every failure to
// read a line -- no legs, a mode that is not octal, a letter the model does not name, an EMPTY group
// token (find prints at least the numeric gid, so a blank there means the line was not printed by the
// walk this model reads), a file claiming the empty path that means "the start point" -- is `none`, and
// the fold refuses a receipt containing one. THE GROUP IS READ HERE RATHER THAN LATER so a receipt
// missing it is a PARSE failure and not a value somebody has to remember to check: an unreadable line has
// no way to reach the fold looking like a granted entry.
fn dashboard_git_subtree_readback_line(line: String) -> DashboardGitSubtreeReadbackLine? {
  let legs: List<String> = line.split(delimiter: " ")
  match legs.first() {
    Absent => none
    Present { value: letter } =>
      match legs.skip(n: 1).first() {
        Absent => none
        Present { value: printed_mode } =>
          match legs.skip(n: 2).first() {
            Absent => none
            Present { value: printed_group } =>
              if trim(s: printed_group) == "" {
                none
              } else {
                match file_mode_of_octal_text(text: trim(s: printed_mode)) {
                  Absent => none
                  Present { value: mode } =>
                    {
                      let path = join(legs.skip(n: 3), " ")
                      let group = trim(s: printed_group) as NonEmptyStr
                      if letter == (find_printf_type_directory_letter as String) {
                        if path == "" {
                          Present { value: ReadbackSubtreeTop { mode: mode, group: group } }
                        } else {
                          Present {
                            value: ReadbackInteriorDirectory {
                              mode: mode,
                              group: group,
                              path: path as NonEmptyStr,
                            },
                          }
                        }
                      } else if letter == (find_printf_type_regular_file_letter as String) {
                        if path == "" {
                          none
                        } else {
                          Present {
                            value: ReadbackEntryFile {
                              mode: mode,
                              group: group,
                              path: path as NonEmptyStr,
                            },
                          }
                        }
                      } else {
                        none
                      }
                    }
                }
              }
          }
      }
  }
}

// DID THE READBACK PROVE THE SUBTREE GRANTED? One fold over the walk's receipt, so the apply's refusal
// and the claims' assertion are the SAME decision over the same figure -- a second copy of this
// reasoning is exactly the drift the §3 rounds on this PR kept removing.
//
// WHAT EACH CASE IS DECIDED UNDER is written at the arm that decides it, because the three cases are a
// declared sum (DashboardGitSubtreeReadbackLine) rather than booleans to be tested in an order.
//  - an UNPARSEABLE mode text refuses the receipt. A walk that printed something the model cannot read
//    has not proved a grant, and treating it as one is the fabricated output DESIGN 5 forbids.
//  - an EMPTY receipt refuses. A walk that matched nothing proves nothing about an existing tree --
//    which is the class of failure this whole follow-up exists to kill (see the recurring failure mode
//    directory_ownership_assertion_stops_at_the_top_level).
// THE SAME DECISION OVER AN ARBITRARY TOP MODE. The repository subtrees take their exact top mode from
// dashboard_git_worker_subtree_granted_directory_mode; the per-attempt worktree entry -- whose
// subtree top is granted 02770 and which carries NO sticky bit anywhere, see
// dispatch_attempt_worker_git_entry_migration -- is decided under dashboard_git_worker_granted_directory_mode.
// One fold, both callers, so the apply, the spawn and the claims cannot disagree about "granted".
// THE PER-SUBTREE READING OF THE FOLD: the caller says WHICH SUBTREE, and the top mode and the
// files-are-replaced decision are derived from the rows that declare it, so a caller cannot grade objects/
// against the refs/ shape. THE GROUP IS SUPPLIED, NOT READ HERE (no default, DESIGN 5): the fold compares
// find's `%g` against the token the grant was told, and only the caller knows which token its host
// resolves -- production binds fleet_posix_worker_shared_group_name at the two places that grant and prove
// for real (roadmap_dashboard_instance_apply's refusal, roadmap_belt_actuate's spawn gate) and the dry
// pairing claim the_production_route_names_the_declared_group_in_every_grant_command keeps that production
// binding executed and asserted. A witness grouping a throwaway tree to a token its own host resolves
// passes that token here, and the substitution is stated in place.
fn dashboard_git_subtree_readback_is_granted(
  child: NonEmptyStr,
  group: NonEmptyStr,
  receipt: String,
) -> Bool {
  dashboard_git_subtree_readback_is_granted_under(
    top: dashboard_git_worker_subtree_granted_directory_mode(child: child),
    group: group,
    files_group_writable: dashboard_git_worker_subtree_writes_files_in_place(child: child),
    receipt: receipt,
  )
}

fn dashboard_git_subtree_readback_is_granted_under(
  top: FileMode,
  group: NonEmptyStr,
  files_group_writable: Bool,
  receipt: String,
) -> Bool {
  let lines = filter(split(s: receipt, delimiter: "\n"), l => trim(s: l) != "")
  let parsed = map(lines, l => dashboard_git_subtree_readback_line(line: l))
  length(parsed) > 0
    // an unreadable line is a refusal, not a line to be argued about;
    && all(parsed, entry => match entry {
      Absent => false
      Present { value: _ } => true
    })
    // and the subtree's OWN directory has to be in the receipt: a walk that matched only entries below it
    // says nothing about the mode that decides whether tomorrow's fan-out directory can be created.
    && any(parsed, entry => match entry {
      Absent => false
      Present { value: line } => match line {
        ReadbackSubtreeTop { mode: _, group: _ } => true
        ReadbackInteriorDirectory { mode: _, group: _, path: _ } => false
        ReadbackEntryFile { mode: _, group: _, path: _ } => false
      }
    })
    // THE GROUP HALF OF THE GRANT, ON EVERY LINE. The migration's first command is a chgrp, and a receipt
    // that printed only modes would certify a tree whose chgrp never took -- including the state this
    // whole slice exists to fix, where the subtree sits at 0775 with the WRONG GROUP and every mode a
    // fold that cannot see groups calls correct. Compared as TEXT against the token the chgrp was told,
    // because that is the same string the grant used: `chgrp 1003` and `find %g` both print 1003, and
    // `chgrp gunbc-attempt` reads back as `gunbc-attempt` on a host that has the name. A host that groups
    // by number and reads a number is the same comparison; the fold does not care which spelling the
    // caller and the tool traded in, only that the entry's group IS what the grant was told to set.
    && all(parsed, entry => match entry {
      Absent => false
      Present { value: line } => (line_group_of(line) as String) == (group as String)
    })
    && all(parsed, entry => match entry {
      Absent => false
      Present { value: line } => match line {
        // The TOP is held EXACT to the declared arm this subtree's one authority row picks
        // (dashboard_git_worker_subtree_declared_directory_mode): 03770 for objects/, 02770 for refs/ and
        // logs/, other --- in both. Nothing lenient here, and nothing hand-spelled: `top` arrives rendered
        // from that row, so the install that owns the top and the readback that certifies it cannot disagree
        // -- round-3 finding (4) is precisely what an exact top leg protects (an assert-then-sweep route
        // that lands at some third mode must REFUSE, not be accepted by a window).
        ReadbackSubtreeTop { mode: mode, group: _ } =>
          dashboard_git_worker_file_modes_are_equal(a: mode, b: top)
        // An INTERIOR directory gets the granted-bits-plus-floor window (see
        // dashboard_git_worker_interior_directory_mode_is_accepted): group-writable, setgid, never
        // world-writable, never setuid -- and the OTHER leg is whatever the host left, because the sweep
        // adds and never clears those bits. A fan-out directory git creates tomorrow inherits setgid from
        // its parent but NOT the sticky bit, so demanding 03770 inside objects/ would refuse a tree that is
        // correctly granted; a 0775 that never went through the walk (no setgid) refuses.
        ReadbackInteriorDirectory { mode: mode, group: _, path: _ } =>
          dashboard_git_worker_interior_directory_mode_is_accepted(mode: mode)
        // A FILE's leg depends on what git does to that subtree's files, and the two branches refuse
        // each other's shapes: a subtree git only REPLACES entries in (objects, refs) is correctly
        // granted with its files at git's own read-only modes, because a lock-and-rename asks the
        // PARENT for write and the file for nothing -- demanding group-write there would refuse a
        // repository git itself left at 0444 (see dashboard_git_worker_readonly_file_mode_is_accepted,
        // measured). A subtree git APPENDS to (a reflog, an entry's logs/HEAD) must be group-writable,
        // and the 0444 that is right next door would die with "unable to append: Permission denied".
        // BOTH branches refuse world-write and any special bit, so a setgid file -- the shape that
        // turned a group grant into a privilege -- passes neither.
        ReadbackEntryFile { mode: mode, group: _, path: _ } =>
          if files_group_writable {
            dashboard_git_worker_file_mode_is_granted(mode: mode)
          } else {
            dashboard_git_worker_readonly_file_mode_is_accepted(mode: mode)
          }
      }
    })
}

// THE GROUP FIELD, READ OFF WHICHEVER CASE THE LINE IS. Its own function rather than three copies of a
// three-way match at the call site: the field is the same fact in all three cases, and a fold that
// spelled the match inline would have to be edited twice if a fourth kind of line were ever printed.
fn line_group_of(line: DashboardGitSubtreeReadbackLine) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match line {
    ReadbackSubtreeTop { mode: _, group: group } => group
    ReadbackInteriorDirectory { mode: _, group: group, path: _ } => group
    ReadbackEntryFile { mode: _, group: group, path: _ } => group
  }
}

// THE BUNDLE INBOX: where a bundle-seeded instance stages its source bundle. Demanded only by an
// instance whose source seed IS a bundle, and the staged bundle path is built under the same function
// (isolated_dashboard_instance), so the inbox ensured and the inbox written are one path.
fn dashboard_inbox_under(root: FilePath) -> FilePath {
  dashboard_instance_child(root: root, child: "inbox")
}

fn dashboard_instance_inbox_directories(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> List<OwnedDirectoryTarget> {
  match instance.source_seed {
    DashboardExistingRepo { anchor_ref: _ } => []
    DashboardGitBundle { controller_repo: _, staged_bundle: _, anchor_ref: _, history_remote: _ } => [
      OwnedDirectoryDeclared {
        member: "bundle-inbox" as NonEmptyStr,
        path: dashboard_inbox_under(root: instance.instance_root),
        owner: instance.directory_owner,
        mode: DirectoryOwnerAllEveryoneTraverse,
        group_adoption: ForeignOwnershipRefuses,
      },
    ]
  }
}

// THIS MODULE'S OWN DEMANDS, in the order the deleted provision plan created them.
// THE DIRECTORIES THAT MAKE A DEDICATED WORKER'S PATHS REACHABLE. The unit runs as gunbc-worker, which
// reaches the instance only through the shared attempt group, so the instance root is the service
// user's, grouped gunbc-attempt, 0750 (group traverses by membership, other gets nothing), and its
// parent -- outside the home tree, where nothing already opens it -- is 0755 so the worker can enter
// it. Created by the live_deploy owned-directory route (gunbc.live_deploy.emit
// owned_directory_apply_steps), observed by dashboard_instance_observe. A service-user instance
// declares neither: its unit enters nothing it does not own.
fn dashboard_instance_provision_parent_directory(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> List<OwnedDirectoryTarget> {
  match instance.worker {
    WorkerIsServiceUser => []
    WorkerDedicatedAccount => [
      OwnedDirectoryDeclared {
        member: "instance-provision-parent" as NonEmptyStr,
        path: instance.provision_parent_root,
        owner: instance.directory_owner,
        mode: DirectoryOwnerAllEveryoneTraverse,
        group_adoption: ForeignOwnershipRefuses,
      },
    ]
  }
}

fn dashboard_instance_root_directory(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> List<OwnedDirectoryTarget> {
  match instance.worker {
    WorkerIsServiceUser => []
    WorkerDedicatedAccount => [
      OwnedDirectoryDeclared {
        member: "instance-root" as NonEmptyStr,
        path: instance.instance_root,
        owner: PosixOwnerNames { user: instance.directory_owner.user, group: fleet_posix_worker_shared_group_name },
        mode: DirectoryOwnerAllGroupTraverse,
        group_adoption: ForeignOwnershipRefuses,
      },
    ]
  }
}

fn dashboard_instance_own_directory_demands(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> List<OwnedDirectoryTarget> {
  concat(concat(dashboard_instance_provision_parent_directory(instance: instance), dashboard_instance_root_directory(instance: instance)), concat([
    dashboard_instance_dispatch_worktree_directory(instance: instance),
    dashboard_instance_dispatch_attempt_state_directory(instance: instance),
    dashboard_instance_provider_state_directory(instance: instance),
  ], concat(dashboard_instance_worker_git_directories(instance: instance), dashboard_instance_inbox_directories(instance: instance))))
}
fn dashboard_instance_dispatch_state_paths(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> List<FilePath> {
  [
    dashboard_instance_dispatch_worktree_root(instance: instance),
    dashboard_instance_dispatch_attempt_state_root(instance: instance),
  ]
}

fn dashboard_paths_all_under(paths: List<FilePath>, root: FilePath) -> Bool {
  all(paths, path => dashboard_path_is_under(path: path, root: root))
}

fn dashboard_instance_paths_are_owned(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> Bool {
  dashboard_paths_all_under(
    paths: dashboard_instance_footprint_paths(instance: instance),
    root: instance.instance_root,
  )
    && match instance.dispatch_state_scope {
      DispatchStateInstanceOwned { worktree_child: _, attempt_state_child: _ } =>
        dashboard_paths_all_under(
          paths: dashboard_instance_dispatch_state_paths(instance: instance),
          root: instance.instance_root,
        )
      DispatchStateHostShared { worktree_root: _, attempt_state_root: _ } => true
    }
}

fn dashboard_paths_are_disjoint(a: List<FilePath>, b: List<FilePath>) -> Bool {
  all(
    a,
    ap => all(
      b,
      bp =>
        ap != bp
          && !dashboard_path_is_under(path: ap, root: bp)
          && !dashboard_path_is_under(path: bp, root: ap),
    ),
  )
}

fn dashboard_instances_have_disjoint_owned_paths(
  a: HostDashboardInstance,
  b: HostDashboardInstance,
) -> Bool {
  dashboard_paths_are_disjoint(
    a: dashboard_instance_footprint_paths(instance: a),
    b: dashboard_instance_footprint_paths(instance: b),
  )
    && dashboard_instances_dispatch_state_is_consistent(a: a, b: b)
}

// THE REPLACEMENT FOR BLANKET DISJOINTNESS IS AGREEMENT, NOT ABSENCE. Two production slots on one
// host must resolve dispatch state to the SAME roots, and that is a checkable claim rather than a
// hole where a check used to be — a slot given its own attempt-state root fails this, which is
// precisely the shape that put a teardown of live dispatch evidence on the per-push path.\n\nTHE
// DIFFERENT-HOST ARM IS EXPLICIT BECAUSE THE FIRST CUT GOT IT WRONG (codex review 47405). This note
// already argued that two host-shared instances on different machines describe different
// directories and that disjointness is not the question their scope asks — and then the code routed
// exactly that pair into the disjointness branch anyway, where srv1 and srv2 layout paths, which
// gunbc_host_layout_note states are byte-identical by convention, would compare EQUAL and refuse a
// legitimate configuration. The prose was right and the fold contradicted it, which is the §3
// stale-citation class turned inward: no live pair exercised it, so it stayed green. The arm now
// returns a valid outcome by name rather than by falling through.\n\nThe remaining pairings say
// which is which rather than defaulting: a host-shared instance beside an instance-owned one is a
// production slot beside a lab, and the lab exists to keep its dispatch evidence away from
// production, so disjointness is exactly right there.\n\nThis is the pairwise law. The population
// law it serves — every declared instance is path-consistent with every other — is
// dashboard_declared_instances_owned_paths_are_pairwise_consistent, which folds this predicate
// over public_builtin_dashboard_instances rather than asking a witness to name the pairs.

fn dashboard_instances_dispatch_state_is_consistent(
  a: HostDashboardInstance,
  b: HostDashboardInstance,
) -> Bool {
  match a.dispatch_state_scope {
    DispatchStateInstanceOwned { worktree_child: _, attempt_state_child: _ } =>
      dashboard_paths_are_disjoint(
        a: dashboard_instance_dispatch_state_paths(instance: a),
        b: dashboard_instance_dispatch_state_paths(instance: b),
      )
    DispatchStateHostShared { worktree_root: a_worktree, attempt_state_root: a_attempts } =>
      match b.dispatch_state_scope {
        DispatchStateInstanceOwned { worktree_child: _, attempt_state_child: _ } =>
          dashboard_paths_are_disjoint(
            a: dashboard_instance_dispatch_state_paths(instance: a),
            b: dashboard_instance_dispatch_state_paths(instance: b),
          )
        DispatchStateHostShared { worktree_root: b_worktree, attempt_state_root: b_attempts } =>
          if a.host_identity == b.host_identity {
            a_worktree == b_worktree && a_attempts == b_attempts
          } else {
            true
          }
      }
  }
}

// NOTHING INSTALLED A LAB'S BINARY, BECAUSE LABS USED TO BORROW PRODUCTION'S. Before serve_binary
// became instance-derived, every isolated instance resolved it to the host layout's
// /opt/gunbc/bin/gunbc — the file production is executing. That is why the plan had no install
// step: there was nothing to install, only something to point at. It is also exactly the hazard the
// twin witness caught, since ServeBinary is an Owned deployment artifact and a lab retract would
// have removed the binary out from under the running service.\n\nSo closing that hazard opened this
// gap, and the two belong in one change. An instance that owns its binary needs a step that puts
// one there, or the dashboard apply refuses at target-dependencies against a path only it was ever
// going to create — a chain that terminates in nobody's responsibility.\n\nIt copies rather than
// builds. The controller's already-built target/release/gunbc is the source, staged and moved into
// place through the same atomic copy the source bundle uses, so a half-written binary is never
// observable at the destination. Building is deliberately NOT modeled here: a provisioner that
// silently compiled would make apply's duration and its toolchain requirements invisible, and a
// stale or missing build should refuse with the path it looked for rather than quietly producing
// one that differs from what the operator tested.

// THE PLAN CREATES NO DIRECTORY. Every directory an instance's consumers write into -- the dispatch
// roots, the provider state root DashboardVerifyProviderRuntime and DashboardVerifyProviderAuth read,
// the auth stores, the bundle inbox, the event worktree -- is a demand its consumer declares, composed
// by gunbc.roadmap.dashboard_instance_directories dashboard_instance_directory_demands and ensured by
// the one owned-directory provider (gunbc.owned_directory owned_directory_ensure) BEFORE this plan
// runs (gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_dashboard_instance_apply dashboard_apply_directories_then_plan). The two
// directory ops this type carried were a second creator beside live_deploy's, with a different
// meaning: a mkdir as whoever ran it, with no owner, no mode and no readback of most of what it made.

type DashboardInstanceProvisionOp
  = DashboardHydrateControllerHistory {
      controller_repo: FilePath,
      history_remote: NonEmptyStr,
    }
  | DashboardPrepareGitBundle {
      controller_repo: FilePath,
      anchor_ref: NonEmptyStr,
    }
  | DashboardStageGitBundle {
      controller_repo: FilePath,
      staged_bundle: FilePath,
    }
  | DashboardEnsureServeBinary {
      controller_repo: FilePath,
      built_binary: FilePath,
      path: FilePath,
    }
  | DashboardEnsureOwnershipMarker {
      path: FilePath,
      instance_id: NonEmptyStr,
    }
  | DashboardEnsureLocalOrigin {
      bundle: FilePath,
      origin: FilePath,
      source_ref: NonEmptyStr,
      publish_ref: NonEmptyStr,
    }
  | DashboardEnsureSourceRepo {
      origin: FilePath,
      repo: FilePath,
      anchor_ref: NonEmptyStr,
    }
  | DashboardVerifyProviderRuntime {
      executable: FilePath,
      state_root: FilePath,
    }
  | DashboardVerifyProviderAuth {
      executable: FilePath,
      state_root: FilePath,
    }
  // THE SHARED-REPOSITORY POSTURE, as a declared op (WORKER-ROOT-EQUIVALENT-3). THE ORDER IS THE
  // CLAIM: it runs after the repository exists and BEFORE any attempt's `git worktree add`, because
  // core.sharedRepository governs the modes git gives the private entry's files WHEN IT IS CREATED --
  // set afterwards and the entry arrives 0644/0755 and the group write dies on the worker's first
  // index.lock. Three `git config --local --replace-all` writes made by the instance's own principal
  // (extdeps/git), the only writes this roadmap ever makes to a managed .git/config; the file itself
  // stays read-only to the worker. Empty on the staged arm, so prod gets no op.
  | DashboardEnsureGitSharedRepositoryPosture {
      repo: FilePath,
    }
  // THE ONE-TIME RE-GROUP OF THE SUBTREES AN EXISTING REPOSITORY ALREADY HOLDS, as an op rather than a
  // hand step on the host. WHY AN OP AND NOT AN ASSERTION IN the owned-directory provider: that provider
  // is non-recursive on purpose (DESIGN 4b/6b), and making it recursive would put hooks, info,
  // objects/pack and objects/info -- the children #13942 keeps out of the worker's reach -- inside the
  // path a deploy writes to, and would re-walk an object store on EVERY apply. A bounded `find` sweep
  // with a declared prune group, run once and read back, is the same guarantee with neither cost; and
  // because it is an op, the apply's receipt carries the proof that it happened. Empty on the staged
  // arm, so prod gets no op.
  | DashboardMigrateGitWorkerSubtree {
      repo: FilePath,
      // THE GROUP THE GRANT SETS, CARRIED BY THE OPERATION RATHER THAN INVENTED INSIDE IT. The argv this
      // arm runs are the argv some caller authored, and the token is part of them; the arm also reads the
      // tree back through the fold that compares find's `%g` against it, so the operation that GRANTS and
      // the operation that PROVES must carry one value rather than each read the row and risk disagreeing.
      // THE PRODUCTION PLAN SITE BINDS fleet_posix_worker_shared_group_name -- the row fleet_posix_accounts
      // declares, and the account creation this slice is built beside -- so no production route invents a
      // name; a witness on a host that has no such group supplies the token it can resolve. NO DEFAULT: a
      // default would let a call site silently take the production name where it meant to name the host's,
      // which is a fabricated value (DESIGN 5). The dry pairing claim
      // the_production_route_names_the_declared_group_in_every_grant_command asserts the PRODUCTION route
      // -- the plan through to the argv -- names the declared group, so the supplied value is never the
      // only route that executes (DESIGN 3 pairing).
      group: NonEmptyStr,
    }
  // THE ONE-TIME MOVE OF A STORE'S FLAT ENTRIES OUT OF THE SHARED DISPATCH ATTEMPT-STATE ROOT INTO THE
  // MEMBER THE STORE NOW OWNS (gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_attempt_state_layout_move). The same op for every
  // store: the row carries the member and the legacy pattern, and nothing here names a store. It is the deploy
  // leg of the cutover: the store's mint refuses permanently while a legacy entry remains, so this op runs
  // before the new code starts or the store stays unready. Planned and read back like the git subtree
  // re-group above; a second run finds nothing to move (Noop) and a collision refuses.
  | DashboardMoveAttemptStateEntries {
      row: MoveRow,
    }

// live_deploy systemd-launches the dashboard process only for deployment_spec_srv1 (srv1_live);
// deployment_spec_for has no production caller. This plan does not start the process and does not
// poll health after launch: production process start is live_deploy ServiceProcess, and for every
// other instance start is a declared operator act until deployment_spec_for is a real caller.
//
// DIRECTORIES ARE NOT IN THIS PLAN FOR ANY INSTANCE. Both routes -- this apply for srv1_lab,
// srv2_lab, srv2_deploy and macbook_local, and the live_deploy transaction for srv1_live -- ensure the
// same demand list through the same provider, so no instance depends on this plan to create a
// directory and no instance is left without a creator by its removal.
fn dashboard_instance_provision_plan(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> List<DashboardInstanceProvisionOp> {
  let controller_source_ops = match instance.source_seed {
    DashboardExistingRepo { anchor_ref: _ } => []
    DashboardGitBundle {
      controller_repo,
      staged_bundle: _,
      anchor_ref,
      history_remote,
    } => [
      DashboardHydrateControllerHistory {
        controller_repo: controller_repo,
        history_remote: history_remote,
      },
      DashboardPrepareGitBundle {
        controller_repo: controller_repo,
        anchor_ref: anchor_ref,
      },
    ]
  }
  let shared_git_posture = match instance.worker {
    WorkerIsServiceUser => []
    WorkerDedicatedAccount => [
      DashboardEnsureGitSharedRepositoryPosture { repo: instance.repo_root },
      // THE PRODUCTION BINDING, AT THE ONLY PLACE THIS OPERATION IS CONSTRUCTED FOR REAL: the declared
      // group, from the row that declares it. Nothing downstream chooses a group name again.
      DashboardMigrateGitWorkerSubtree {
        repo: instance.repo_root,
        group: fleet_posix_worker_shared_group_name,
      },
    ]
  }
  let common = [
    DashboardEnsureServeBinary {
      controller_repo: dashboard_instance_controller_workdir(instance: instance),
      built_binary: dashboard_instance_child(
        root: dashboard_instance_controller_workdir(instance: instance),
        child: join([cargo_default_release_dir_rel, "/gunbc"], ""),
      ),
      path: instance.serve_binary,
    },
    DashboardEnsureOwnershipMarker {
      path: dashboard_instance_ownership_marker_path(instance: instance),
      instance_id: instance.instance_id,
    },
  ]
  let source_ops = match instance.source_seed {
    DashboardExistingRepo { anchor_ref: _ } => []
    DashboardGitBundle {
      controller_repo,
      staged_bundle,
      anchor_ref,
      history_remote: _,
    } => [
      DashboardStageGitBundle {
        controller_repo: controller_repo,
        staged_bundle: staged_bundle,
      },
      DashboardEnsureLocalOrigin {
        bundle: staged_bundle,
        origin: instance.local_origin_root,
        source_ref: anchor_ref,
        publish_ref: instance.local_origin_publish_ref,
      },
      DashboardEnsureSourceRepo {
        origin: instance.local_origin_root,
        repo: instance.repo_root,
        anchor_ref: instance.dispatch_base_ref,
      },
    ]
  }
  let attempt_state_moves = map(attempt_state_move_rows, row => DashboardMoveAttemptStateEntries { row: row })
  concat(attempt_state_moves, concat(controller_source_ops, concat(common, concat(source_ops, concat(
    shared_git_posture,
    dashboard_instance_provider_verification_ops(instance: instance),
  )))))
}

fn dashboard_instance_provider_verification_ops(
  instance: HostDashboardInstance,
) -> List<DashboardInstanceProvisionOp> {
  match dashboard_instance_provider_executable(instance: instance) {
    Present { value: executable } => [
      DashboardVerifyProviderRuntime {
        executable: executable,
        state_root: dashboard_instance_provider_state_root(instance: instance),
      },
      DashboardVerifyProviderAuth {
        executable: executable,
        state_root: dashboard_instance_provider_state_root(instance: instance),
      },
    ]
    Absent => []
  }
}

type DashboardInstanceProvisionPlanRow {
  kind: NonEmptyStr
  target: String
  detail: String
}

fn dashboard_instance_provision_op_row(
  op: DashboardInstanceProvisionOp,
) -> DashboardInstanceProvisionPlanRow {
  match op {
    DashboardHydrateControllerHistory { controller_repo, history_remote } =>
      DashboardInstanceProvisionPlanRow {
        kind: "hydrate-controller-history" as NonEmptyStr,
        target: controller_repo as String,
        detail: join([
          "complete shallow history from named remote ",
          history_remote as String,
          "; no-op when already complete",
        ], ""),
      }
    DashboardPrepareGitBundle { controller_repo, anchor_ref } =>
      DashboardInstanceProvisionPlanRow {
        kind: "prepare-git-bundle" as NonEmptyStr,
        target: controller_repo as String,
        detail: join([
          anchor_ref as String,
          " -> temporary bundle -> empty bare repository import proof",
        ], ""),
      }
    DashboardStageGitBundle { controller_repo: _, staged_bundle } =>
      DashboardInstanceProvisionPlanRow {
        kind: "stage-git-bundle" as NonEmptyStr,
        target: staged_bundle as String,
        detail: "copy proven temporary bundle to .next, then atomically rename",
      }
    DashboardEnsureServeBinary { controller_repo: _, built_binary, path } =>
      DashboardInstanceProvisionPlanRow {
        kind: "ensure-serve-binary" as NonEmptyStr,
        target: path as String,
        detail: join([
          "copy the controller's built seed from ",
          built_binary as String,
          "; atomic move, never a build",
        ], ""),
      }
    DashboardEnsureOwnershipMarker { path, instance_id } =>
      DashboardInstanceProvisionPlanRow {
        kind: "ensure-ownership-marker" as NonEmptyStr,
        target: path as String,
        detail: instance_id as String,
      }
    DashboardEnsureLocalOrigin { bundle, origin, source_ref, publish_ref } =>
      DashboardInstanceProvisionPlanRow {
        kind: "ensure-local-origin" as NonEmptyStr,
        target: origin as String,
        detail: join([
          bundle as String,
          " ",
          source_ref as String,
          " -> ",
          publish_ref as String,
        ], ""),
      }
    DashboardEnsureSourceRepo { origin, repo, anchor_ref } =>
      DashboardInstanceProvisionPlanRow {
        kind: "ensure-source-repo" as NonEmptyStr,
        target: repo as String,
        detail: join([origin as String, " @ ", anchor_ref as String], ""),
      }
    DashboardVerifyProviderRuntime { executable, state_root } =>
      DashboardInstanceProvisionPlanRow {
        kind: "verify-provider-runtime" as NonEmptyStr,
        target: executable as String,
        detail: join([
          "run --version through the modeled environment binding with CODEX_HOME=",
          state_root as String,
          "; require codex-cli identity",
        ], ""),
      }
    DashboardVerifyProviderAuth { executable, state_root } =>
      DashboardInstanceProvisionPlanRow {
        kind: "verify-provider-auth" as NonEmptyStr,
        target: state_root as String,
        detail: executable as String,
      }
    DashboardEnsureGitSharedRepositoryPosture { repo } =>
      DashboardInstanceProvisionPlanRow {
        kind: "ensure-git-shared-repository-posture" as NonEmptyStr,
        target: repo as String,
        detail: "core.sharedRepository=group, gc.auto=0, maintenance.auto=false; run by the instance principal, before any attempt worktree is added",
      }
    DashboardMigrateGitWorkerSubtree { repo: repo, group: group } =>
      DashboardInstanceProvisionPlanRow {
        kind: "migrate-git-worker-subtree" as NonEmptyStr,
        target: repo as String,
        detail: "bounded re-group of objects/, refs/ and logs/ to the attempt group with g+w and setgid (objects/ also +t); confined children pruned; read back path by path before the receipt",
      }
    DashboardMoveAttemptStateEntries { row: row } =>
      DashboardInstanceProvisionPlanRow {
        kind: "move-attempt-state-entries" as NonEmptyStr,
        target: row.member as String,
        detail: join(["move the flat ", row.legacy_flat_prefix as String, "* entries of the dispatch attempt-state root into the member ", row.member as String, "; Noop when none remain, refused on a collision, read back before the receipt"], ""),
      }
  }
}

fn dashboard_instance_provision_plan_rows(
  instance: HostDashboardInstance,
) -> List<DashboardInstanceProvisionPlanRow> {
  map(
    dashboard_instance_provision_plan(instance: instance),
    op => dashboard_instance_provision_op_row(op: op),
  )
}

// A REFUSED PROBE IS NOT A NEGATIVE FACT, and every Bool in this record used to say it was.
// dashboard_target_path_present reduced its probe to .success, so an SSH transport refusal, a
// missing test binary, or an unreachable host all arrived here as 'the path is absent' — and the
// same collapse fed provider_runtime_ready and provider_authenticated, so a broken transport
// reported as an unauthenticated provider. The SSH arm even HAD the distinction:
// typed_argv_probe_over_ssh returns TypedArgvExecRefused with a reason, which was thrown away
// converting to the two-state carrier (review, 2026-07-30).\n\nThat is the fabricated-verdict class
// this session kept producing at every layer — a missing cargo reported as rustfmt drift, an idle
// socket as a malformed request, a foreign server's health as convergence. Here it is worse than a
// wrong label, because the wrong label is ACTIONABLE: an operator told the state root is absent
// creates it, when the truth is that nothing could be observed at all.\n\nThe refusals ride
// ALONGSIDE the fields rather than converting each Bool to a tri-state, because a transport failure
// does not invalidate one field — it invalidates the whole observation. Ten probes feed one record;
// if any could not run, no Bool here is evidence of anything, and consumers must refuse on the
// channel BEFORE reading a single one. Per-field tri-states would let a caller keep trusting the
// nine that happened to answer, which is exactly the reasoning that makes a partial observation
// look complete.

type DashboardInstanceObserved {
  observation_refusals: List<String>
  root_present: Bool
  ownership_marker_present: Bool
  bundle_present: Bool
  origin_present: Bool
  source_present: Bool
  source_revision_observed: Bool
  source_revision_current: Bool
  source_revision_detail: String
  directories_converged: Bool
  directory_refusals: List<String>
  provider_runtime_ready: Bool
  provider_runtime_detail: String
  provider_authenticated: Bool
  provider_auth_detail: String
  health_current: Bool
  production_untouched: Bool
  source_anchor_matches: Bool
}

// Source convergence has two independent equalities. source_anchor_matches compares the target
// checkout HEAD with its target-local origin/base ref; that proves the owned checkout is cleanly
// resettable and prevents overwriting a divergent worktree. source_revision_current compares that
// HEAD with the exact controller CommitSha captured by preflight; that proves the running lab is
// this requested source revision rather than merely self-consistent at an older revision. A clean
// stale revision derives Apply, an unreadable revision refuses, and only both equalities can
// converge. The split is load-bearing: the first lab refresh after a controller rebase exposed that
// HEAD == old origin/main was true while HEAD == requested commit was false.

fn dashboard_source_seed_ready(
  instance: HostDashboardInstance,
  observed: DashboardInstanceObserved,
) -> Bool {
  match instance.source_seed {
    DashboardExistingRepo { anchor_ref: _ } => true
    DashboardGitBundle {
      controller_repo: _,
      staged_bundle: _,
      anchor_ref: _,
      history_remote: _,
    } =>
      observed.bundle_present
  }
}

type DashboardInstanceReconcile
  = DashboardInstanceConverged
  | DashboardInstanceApply { plan: List<DashboardInstanceProvisionOp> }
  | DashboardInstanceRefused { reason: NonEmptyStr }

// THE REFUSAL CHANNEL IS READ BEFORE ANY FIELD, because after a refused probe no field in the record is evidence. Every branch below reasons from Bools — origin present, source present, health current — and each of those is false when the probe could not run just as surely as when the thing is genuinely missing. Reaching them with an unread refusal means deriving a plan from an observation that never happened.\n\nThe order is the whole point. Placing this check second, after the ownership law, would already be too late for anything the ownership law consults; placing it first means a transport failure produces one refusal naming what could not be observed, rather than a confident Apply plan built to create files that may well already exist.\n\nIt refuses rather than retrying or widening. A probe that could not run is not a slow probe, and a reconcile that responded by planning the superset — create everything, it is safe if it already exists — would be the absorbing fallback wearing a plausible justification.
fn dashboard_instance_reconcile(
  instance: HostDashboardInstance,
  observed: DashboardInstanceObserved,
) -> DashboardInstanceReconcile {
  if count(observed.observation_refusals) > 0 {
    DashboardInstanceRefused {
      reason: join([
        "observation incomplete; no field is evidence: ",
        join(observed.observation_refusals, " | "),
      ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else if !dashboard_instance_paths_are_owned(instance: instance) {
    DashboardInstanceRefused {
      reason: "instance-owned paths escape the instance root" as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else if !observed.production_untouched {
    DashboardInstanceRefused {
      reason: "production isolation read-back failed" as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else if count(observed.directory_refusals) > 0 {
    DashboardInstanceRefused {
      reason: join([
        "owned directories refused: ",
        join(observed.directory_refusals, " | "),
      ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else if (observed.origin_present || observed.source_present)
      && !observed.ownership_marker_present {
    DashboardInstanceRefused {
      reason: "source or origin exists without the matching instance ownership marker" as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else if observed.source_present && !observed.source_anchor_matches {
    DashboardInstanceRefused {
      reason: "source repo exists at a different anchor; replacement is destructive and must be adjudicated" as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else if observed.source_present && !observed.source_revision_observed {
    DashboardInstanceRefused {
      reason: join([
        "source revision read-back refused: ",
        observed.source_revision_detail,
      ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else if observed.root_present
      && observed.ownership_marker_present
      && dashboard_source_seed_ready(instance: instance, observed: observed)
      && observed.origin_present
      && observed.source_present
      && observed.directories_converged
      && observed.provider_runtime_ready
      && observed.provider_authenticated
      && observed.source_anchor_matches
      && observed.source_revision_current {
    DashboardInstanceConverged
  } else {
    DashboardInstanceApply { plan: dashboard_instance_provision_plan(instance: instance) }
  }
}

type DashboardInstanceRetractOp
  = DashboardRefuseUnlessAttemptsEmpty { path: FilePath }
  | DashboardRemoveSourceRepo { path: FilePath }
  | DashboardRemoveLocalOrigin { path: FilePath }
  | DashboardPreserveSourceBundle { path: FilePath }

type DashboardInstanceRetract
  = DashboardInstanceRetractPlan { plan: List<DashboardInstanceRetractOp> }
  | DashboardInstanceRetractRefused { reason: NonEmptyStr }

// Retraction is modeled independently from provisioning and is deliberately narrower than recursive
// deletion. Existing-repo instances (including production) are not owned by this model and refuse
// retraction. Isolated bundle-seeded instances refuse while any
// attempt worktree remains, remove the reproducible source clone and local origin, and preserve the
// staged bundle as the recovery anchor. Service stop is live_deploy retract over the systemd
// ServiceProcess member, not a tmux kill in this plan. Provider state is referenced secret
// state and is never in the retract plan.

fn dashboard_instance_retract_plan(
  instance: HostDashboardInstance,
) -> DashboardInstanceRetract {
  if !dashboard_instance_paths_are_owned(instance: instance) {
    DashboardInstanceRetractRefused {
      reason: "instance-owned paths escape the instance root" as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else {
    match instance.source_seed {
      DashboardExistingRepo { anchor_ref: _ } =>
        DashboardInstanceRetractRefused {
          reason: "existing-repo dashboard instances are not owned by the isolated-instance retractor" as NonEmptyStr,
        }
      DashboardGitBundle {
        controller_repo: _,
        staged_bundle,
        anchor_ref: _,
        history_remote: _,
      } =>
        DashboardInstanceRetractPlan { plan: [
          DashboardRefuseUnlessAttemptsEmpty { path: dashboard_instance_dispatch_worktree_root(instance: instance) },
          DashboardRemoveSourceRepo { path: instance.repo_root },
          DashboardRemoveLocalOrigin { path: instance.local_origin_root },
          DashboardPreserveSourceBundle { path: staged_bundle },
        ] }
    }
  }
}
